hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1","In the Name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. My Lord, make easy, complete, aid, and seal with good, O Generous One. Al-Hafiz Abu al-Barakat Abd al-Wahhab ibn al-Mubarak ibn al-Hasan al-Anmati and Abu Ghalib al-Mubarak ibn Abd al-Wahhab ibn Muhammad ibn Mansur al-Qazzaz informed us, both saying: Abu Tahir Ahmad ibn al-Hasan al-Baqillani al-Karaji informed us, saying: Abu Ali al-Hasan ibn Ahmad ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Hasan ibn Shadhan narrated to us, saying: Abu Muhammad Dalaj ibn Ahmad ibn Dalaj al-Sijistani narrated to us, read out to him while I listened, saying: Abu Abd Allah Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Zayd al-Sa'igh narrated to us in the year 291, saying: Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated to us, saying: The Virtues of the Qur'an. 1 - Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us from Abu Ishaq, from Murrah, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Whoever seeks knowledge must adhere to the Qur'an, for in it is the account of the former and latter peoples."
"2","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us from Abu Ishaq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: It will not harm a man not to ask about himself except the Qur'an; if he loves the Qur'an, then he loves Allah the Mighty and Majestic and His Messenger (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"3","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, (who said that) Abdullah said: Whoever loves the Qur'an, let him rejoice."
"4","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: al-Walid ibn Abi Thawr al-Hamdani narrated to us from Abu Husayn, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Learn the Qur'an, for with every letter of it are ten good deeds. I do not say: Alif-Lam-Mim (as one); rather: Alif, and Lam, and Mim."
"5","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us from al-A'mash, from al-Raqashi, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites the Qur'an, it is a richness after which there is no poverty, and trustworthiness is riches."
"6","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, who said: I heard Abu al-Ahwas say: Ibn Mas'ud used to say: Learn the Qur'an and recite it; you will be rewarded ten good deeds for every letter. I do not say: Alif-Lam-Mim; rather: Alif, and Lam, and Mim."
"7","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Shihab narrated to us from Ibrahim al-Hajari, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, who said: This Qur'an is the banquet of Allah, so whoever of you is able to learn something from it, let him do so; for it is the rope of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, the clear light, and the beneficial cure; a protection for whoever holds fast to it, and a salvation for whoever follows it. It does not become crooked so as to be straightened, nor does it deviate so as to be corrected. Its wonders never cease, nor does it wear out from much repetition. Allah the Mighty and Majestic rewards you for its recitation ten good deeds for every letter. I do not say: Alif-Lam-Mim."
"8","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Ziyad ibn Mikhraq narrated to us from Abu Iyas, from Abu Kinanah, who said: Abu Musa said: This Qur'an will be for you a reward, and will be for you a remembrance, and will be against you a burden; so follow the Qur'an and do not let it follow you; for whoever follows the Qur'an, it brings him down to the meadows of Paradise, and whoever is driven by the Qur'an, it pushes him by his neck until it casts him into Hell."
"9","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Layth, from Mujahid, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites a verse from the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, it will be a light for him on the Day of Resurrection, and whoever listens to a verse from the Book of Allah, Allah records for him a multiplied good deed."
"10","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Jarir narrated to us from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, who said: al-Dahhak ibn Qays said: O people, teach your children and your families the Qur'an; for whoever Allah the Mighty and Majestic has decreed, being a Muslim, to enter Paradise, it will be said to him: Recite and ascend the degrees of Paradise, until he reaches the extent of his knowledge of the Qur'an."
"11","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us from al-Awwam, from Ibrahim al-Taymi, who said: It is said to the companion of the Qur'an: Recite, ascend, and recite with measured recitation; and he ends where the Qur'an ends with him."
"12","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us from al-Awwam, from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi', who said: The Qur'an will come on the Day of Resurrection as an obeyed intercessor and a confirmed prosecutor; it will intercede for its companion, saying: O Lord, recompense him, for he acted by me, kept vigil by night with me, and exerted himself with me; so recompense him. Then it will be said: The robe of honor. Then it will say: O Lord, recompense him, for he acted by me, kept vigil with me, and exerted himself with me; so recompense him. Then it will be said: The crown of honor. Then it will say: O Lord, recompense him, for he acted by me, kept vigil with me, and exerted himself with me. He said: Then it will be said: My pleasure, after which there is no anger. He said: To that the intercession of the Qur'an extends."
"13","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us from al-Awwam, from Uqbah ibn Su'ayr, who said: I heard Abu Salih say: That I had gathered the Qur'an and then stood with it for a year would be dearer to me than such-and-such; for it has reached me that it is said to the companion of the Qur'an: Recite, ascend, and recite with measured recitation; and it is hoped that if one has gathered the Qur'an, he will be among those brought near."
"14","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Qatadah, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Sa'd ibn Hisham al-Ansari, from A'ishah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The example of one who recites the Qur'an and memorizes it is like that of the noble righteous scribes; and the example of one who recites it without memorizing, while it is hard upon him and he keeps at it, is that he has two rewards."
"15","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, who said: The one for whom recitation of the Qur'an is easy is recorded among the scribes, and the one for whom its recitation is hard and heavy has two rewards."
"16","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Mansur, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, who said: Keep the Qur'an in mind, for it escapes from the breasts of men faster than camels from their hobbles. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How wretched it is for one of you to say: I forgot verse so-and-so; rather, he was made to forget."
"17","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us from Asim ibn Bahdalah and Mansur, from Abu Wa'il, from Abdullah, who said: How wretched it is for one of you - or he said: for one of them - to say: I forgot verse so-and-so; rather, he was made to forget. Keep reciting the Qur'an, for it escapes from the breasts of men faster than camels from their hobbles - or one of them said: from its hobble."
"18","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Isa ibn Fa'id, from a man, from Sa'd ibn Ubadah, who said: He told him more than once or twice that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no commander of ten but he will be brought on the Day of Resurrection shackled; nothing will free him from his shackle except justice. And whoever recites the Qur'an and then forgets it will meet Allah the Mighty and Majestic maimed."
"19","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us from Luqman ibn Amir, from Suwayd ibn Jabalah al-Fazari, who said: I heard him say: I do not care whether I learn a surah of the Qur'an and then abandon it, or walk among people with my hand cut off."
"20","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: al-Harith ibn Nabhan narrated to us from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Mus'ab ibn Sa'd, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you is he who learns the Qur'an and teaches it. And he took my hand and seated me in this seat of mine, and taught me to recite."
"21","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Alqamah ibn Marthad, who said: I heard Sa'd ibn Ubaydah narrate from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami, from Uthman ibn Affan, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The best of you is he who learns the Qur'an and teaches it. And Abu Abd al-Rahman said: That is what seated me in this seat of mine."
"22","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Amr ibn Murrah, who said: I heard Mujahid say: The Qur'an will intercede for its companion on the Day of Resurrection, saying: O Lord, You placed me in his belly, so I kept his night awake and withheld him from much of his desire, and every worker has a wage. Then He says: Stretch out your hand - or he said: your right hand - and He fills it with His pleasure, and He will never be angry with him after that. Then it is said: Recite and ascend; he is raised a degree for every verse, and a good deed for every verse."
"23","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari, from al-Qasim Abu Abd al-Rahman, from Fadalah ibn Ubayd and Tamim al-Dari, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Whoever recites ten verses in a night is recorded among those who pray and is not recorded among the heedless; whoever recites fifty verses is recorded among the memorizers until morning; whoever recites three hundred verses, the Almighty says: My servant has exerted himself for Me; whoever recites a thousand verses, there is recorded for him a qintar - and the qintar is better than the world and all it contains - and more, whatever reward He wills. When the Day of Resurrection comes, your Lord says to the servant: Recite and ascend a degree with every verse, until he reaches the last verse with him. Your Lord says to the servant: Seize. The servant says by his hand: O Lord, You know best. He says: With this, eternity; and with this, bliss."
"24","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Awanah narrated to us from Abu Ishaq, from a man, from Ibn Umar, who said: Whoever recites ten verses in a night is not recorded among the heedless."
"25","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Maryam, who said: Some of our elders narrated to us that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites the Qur'an pronouncing it with correct Arabic inflection, and dies upon that, is like the martyr rolling in his blood in the path of Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"26","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Buhayr ibn Sa'd, from Khalid ibn Ma'dan, from Kathir ibn Murrah, from Uqbah ibn Amir al-Juhani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One who recites the Qur'an aloud is like one who gives charity openly, and one who recites the Qur'an secretly is like one who gives charity secretly."
"27","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Duba'i narrated to us from Thabit al-Bunani, from Anas, that when he completed the Qur'an he would gather his family and supplicate."
"28","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Abu Umayyah, from Mujahid, who said: Whoever completes the Qur'an is given a supplication that is not rejected."
"29","Sa'id narrated to us, Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, saying: An elder informed me, saying: Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah have mercy on him, said: Recite the Qur'an with correct Arabic pronunciation, for it is Arabic; and after you there will be peoples who recite it fluently, but they are not the best of you."
"30","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: I heard Ibn al-Munkadir say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to his Companions while they were reciting the Qur'an, and said: Recite, for all of it is the Book of Allah, before there come a people who straighten it as an arrow shaft is straightened, seeking hasty gain and not delayed reward."
"31","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Humayd al-A'raj, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to us while we were reciting the Qur'an, and among us were non-Arabs and Bedouins, and he said: Recite, and all is good; and there will come a people who straighten it as an arrow shaft is straightened, seeking hasty gain and not delayed reward."
"32","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Ubayd Allah ibn Abi Yazid, from his father, from Umm Ayyub, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Qur'an was revealed in seven modes; in whichever mode you recite, you are correct."
"33","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Amr ibn Dinar, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Qur'an was revealed in seven modes, all of them healing and sufficient."
"34","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Abdullah, who said: I have listened to the recitations and found them only close to one another; so recite as you were taught. Beware of affectation and disagreement, for it is only like one of you saying: Come, come hither, come here."
"35","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, who said: I heard al-Nazzal ibn Sabrah narrate from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: I heard a man recite a verse differently from what I had heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so I took him and brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I recognized displeasure on the face of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Both of you do well; do not differ."
"36","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, saying: Abu Imran al-Jawni narrated to us from Abdullah ibn Rabah, from Abdullah ibn Amr - or Umar, Sa'id doubted - who said: I went early to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one day, and he heard two men differing over a verse; he came out with anger visible on his face and said: Those before you perished only through their differing over the Book."
"37","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us from Yazid ibn Hazim, from Sulayman ibn Yasar, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, came upon a people reciting the Qur'an and debating over it, and said: What is this? They said: We recite the Qur'an and debate over it. He said: Debate, but do not make linguistic errors."
"38","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us from Yahya ibn Atiq, who said: I asked al-Hasan about a man learning Arabic to correct his speech and to correct the Qur'an with it, and he said: There is no harm in it; for a man recites a verse and fails to grasp its meaning, and so perishes."
"39","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, who said: Abu Bakr al-Siddiq, may Allah be pleased with him, was asked about a verse from the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, and said: What earth would carry me, or what sky would shade me, or where would I go, and what would I do, if I spoke about a verse of the Book of Allah other than what Allah meant by it?"
"40","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us from Idris, and he was among the best of people, who said: It was said to al-Hasan: We have an imam who makes linguistic errors. He said: Put him back (remove him)."
"41","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Yahya al-Abahh narrated to us from Marwan al-Asfar, who said: I was sitting with Sa'id ibn Jubayr when a man asked him about a verse from the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, and Sa'id said to him: Allah knows best. The man said to him: Say something about it, may Allah keep you right, by your opinion. He said: Should I speak about the Book of Allah by my opinion? He repeated it twice or thrice, and did not answer him with anything."
"42","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: al-Awwam ibn Hawshab narrated to us, saying: Ibrahim al-Taymi narrated to us, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, was alone one day pondering, then sent for Ibn Abbas and said: How will this ummah differ when its Prophet is one, its Book is one, and its qiblah is one? Ibn Abbas said: O Commander of the Believers, the Qur'an was revealed to us, so we recited it and knew concerning what it was revealed. After us there will be peoples who recite the Qur'an but do not know concerning what it was revealed, so every people will have an opinion about it. When every people has an opinion, they will differ; and when they differ, they will fight. Umar rebuked him and scolded him. Ibn Abbas left, then he called him afterwards and recognized what he had said. Then he said: Come on, repeat to me."
"43","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us from Humayd al-Tawil, from Anas ibn Malik, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, recited from the pulpit: {And fruit and grass}; and said: This fruit we know, but what is grass? Then he returned to himself and said: By your life, this is indeed affectation, O Umar."
"44","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us from Ibn Awn, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, who said: I asked Ubaydah about a verse from the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic, and he said: Upon you is fear of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and rectitude, for those who knew concerning what the Qur'an was revealed have gone."
"45","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us from Mansur, from Khaythamah ibn Abi Khaythamah al-Ansari al-Basri, who said: A man used to circumambulate reciting Surah Yusuf, and people gathered around him; when he finished, he begged. Al-Hasan said: I was with Imran ibn Husayn, and he passed by this beggar, stood, and listened to his recitation. When he finished, he begged, and Imran said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return. Take us away, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Whoever recites the Qur'an, let him ask Allah the Mighty and Majestic, for there will come a people who recite the Qur'an begging people thereby."
"46","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hazm ibn Abi Hazm narrated to us, saying: I heard al-Hasan say: It has reached me that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites one hundred verses in a night, the standing of a night is recorded for him; whoever recites two hundred verses, the Qur'an will not dispute against him; whoever recites five hundred verses, a qintar of reward comes to him by morning, and the qintar is twelve thousand."
"47","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Abd al-Karim al-Basri, from Tawus, that he said: By Allah, I have not seen anyone with better recitation than Talq ibn Habib. And he gestured with his hand. And he was asked: Who recites best among people? He said: The one when you hear his recitation you see that he fears Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"48","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu al-Safar, who said: Hudhayfah said: We are a people who were given faith before being given the Qur'an, and you are a people given the Qur'an before being given faith."
"49","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Mis'ar, from Ma'n, who said: Abdullah said: Allah does not disappoint a house into which a man retires with Surah al-Baqarah or Al Imran, or some of their companions."
"50","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Mis'ar, who said: A man came to Abdullah and said: Advise me. He said: When you hear Allah the Mighty and Majestic say in His Book: O you who believe - lend it your ear, for it is either good you are commanded, or evil you are turned away from."
"51","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from al-Mas'udi, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Recite to me. Abdullah said to him: Shall I recite to you when it was revealed to you? He said: I love to hear it from other than me. So Abdullah recited Surah al-Nisa to him, until when he reached: {How will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness}, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept, and Abdullah stopped."
"52","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Abu al-Duha, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abdullah: Recite. He said: O Messenger of Allah, how shall I recite to you when it was revealed to you? He said: I love to hear it from other than me. Abdullah opened Surah al-Nisa and recited until he reached: {How will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness}; his eyes shed tears, and he said: Enough."
"53","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Ibrahim ibn Sulayman, tutor of Abu Abd Allah, narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Abidah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Abdullah: Recite to me. He said: Shall I recite to you when it was revealed to you? He said: I love to hear it from other than me. So he recited Surah al-Nisa until he ended at His saying: {How will it be when We bring from every nation a witness and bring you against these as a witness}. He said: He nudged me, and I looked, and his tears were flowing down."
"54","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: Alqamah recited to Abdullah, and he had a beautiful voice, and Abdullah said: Recite with measured recitation, may my father and mother be ransom for you, for it is the adornment of the Qur'an."
"55","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us from Ayyub and Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, that Jibril, peace be upon him, and Mika'il descended upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); Mika'il said to him: Recite upon one mode. Jibril said to him: Ask him for more. So he asked for more, and he said to him: Recite upon two modes. He said to him: Ask him for more. So he said to him: Recite upon three modes. He kept asking for more until it reached seven modes, and he said: Recite upon seven modes. Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent, and he fell silent."
"56","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, saying: Husayn narrated to us from Hilal ibn Yasaf, from Abu Hayyan al-Ashja'i, who said: A man met Abdullah and said to him: Recite to me. Ibn Mas'ud said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Recite to me. I said: O Messenger of Allah, did I not learn it from you? He said: Yes, but I love to hear it from other than me."
"57","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Mansur ibn Zadhdhan narrated to us from Ibn Sirin, who said: Jibril used to review the Qur'an with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in every Ramadan; in the year in which he died, he reviewed it with him twice. Ibn Sirin said: So it is hoped that this recitation of ours is upon the last review."
"58","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Abu Zibyan, from Ibn Abbas, who said: He said to me: Which of the two recitations do you count first? We said: Our recitation. He said: No, rather the recitation of Ibn Mas'ud; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have the Qur'an presented to him every Ramadan, and in the year in which he died it was presented to him twice, so Ibn Mas'ud witnessed what of it was abrogated and what was changed."
"59","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us from Ayyub, from Muhammad, who said: I was informed that Ibn Mas'ud used to say: If I knew anyone reachable by camel who had a more recent knowledge of the last review than me, I would go to him - or: I would take trouble to go to him."
"60","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Abu Ammar, from Hudhayfah, who said: Peoples will recite the Qur'an establishing it as an arrow shaft is established, not omitting an alif from it, while their faith does not pass their throats."
"61","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from Mujahid, who said: I used to challenge people in memorization; then I prayed behind Maslamah ibn Mukhallad, and he recited Surah al-Baqarah, omitting neither an alif nor a waw."
"62","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Amr ibn Dinar, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, who said: Ibn Mas'ud said: The Qur'an is masculine, so refer to it in the masculine."
"63","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, saying: Dawud narrated to us from al-Sha'bi, who said: Ibn Mas'ud said: The Qur'an is masculine, so refer to it in the masculine; and if you differ over ya and ta, make it ya."
"64","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Abu Bakr ibn Abd Allah ibn Abi Maryam, who said: I heard Atiyyah ibn Qays and our elders say: If you differ over the recitation of ya and ta, recite with ya, and refer to the Qur'an in the masculine, for it is masculine. Abu Bakr said: And I heard our elders say: Ya is general, and ta is specific."
"65","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Malik ibn Abd Allah al-Kala'i, who said: I heard Khalid ibn Ma'dan say: If you differ over the recitation of ya and ta, recite with ya, and refer to the Qur'an in the masculine, for it is masculine."
"66","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Shu'ayb ibn Dinar, who said: I heard Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir say: Recitation of the Qur'an is a sunnah; the later takes it from the earlier."
"67","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us from his father, from Kharijah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Thabit, who said: Recitation is a sunnah."
"68","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Tammam ibn Najih, from al-Hasan, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes a third of the Qur'an and acts by it has taken the matter of a third of prophethood; whoever takes half the Qur'an has taken the matter of half of prophethood; and whoever takes the whole Qur'an and acts by it has taken all of prophethood."
"69","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, saying: Amr ibn Abi Amr narrated to us from Habib ibn Hind, from Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever takes the seven long surahs of the Qur'an is good."
"70","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Ubayd Allah ibn Ubayd al-Kala'i, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to say: Recite the Qur'an with correct Arabic pronunciation, for it is Arabic; understand the Sunnah; interpret dreams well; and when one of you narrates to his brother, let him say: O Allah, if it is good, it is for us, and if it is evil, it is upon our enemy."
"71","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, who said: Recite the Qur'an with correct Arabic pronunciation."
"72","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Abd al-Aziz ibn Ubayd Allah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah the Mighty and Majestic revealed this Qur'an commanding and forbidding, as a past practice, and a struck parable; in it is your account, the account of those before you, the report of those after you, and the judgment of what is between you. Whoever speaks by it is truthful; whoever disputes by it prevails; whoever acts by it is rewarded; whoever holds fast to it is guided to a straight path. Lengthy repetition does not wear it out, and its wonders never cease."
"73","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, saying: Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us from al-Musayyab ibn Rafi', or another - Hammad doubted - who said: Whoever recites: {When the earth is shaken} it is as if he recited half the Qur'an; and whoever recites: {Say: O disbelievers}; and whoever recites: {Say: He is Allah, One} it is as if he recited a third of the Qur'an."
"74","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Mundhir al-Thawri, from al-Rabi' ibn Khuthaym, from Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, who said: Whoever recites {Say: He is Allah, One} it equals a third of the Qur'an for him."
"75","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Asid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Khath'ami, from Hassan ibn Atiyyah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites: {Ya Sin} it is as if he recited the Qur'an ten times."
"76","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, saying: Abu Sinan narrated to us from Ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, who said: When one of you recites a verse, let him not cut it off until he completes it."
"77","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Ibn Abi Labid, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, or another, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) employed a young man; they seemed to speak about him, though he had recited the Qur'an, and he said: The example of the Qur'an is like a bag filled with musk; if you open it, you open something fragrant, and if you keep it closed, you keep something fragrant."
"78","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us from Abu Hashim, from Ibrahim, concerning His saying the Mighty and Majestic: {Indeed We sent it down in a blessed night}; he said: The Qur'an was sent down entire upon Jibril, peace be upon him, and Jibril would afterwards come to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"79","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Husayn, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: The Qur'an came down entire from the highest heaven to the lowest heaven on the Night of Decree, then came down in portions."
"80","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Malik ibn Shaddad al-Huna'i narrated to us from Abd al-Aziz ibn Sulayman, who said: Abu Hakimah al-Abdi informed me, saying: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came to me while I was writing a mushaf, and kept looking at my writing, and said: Trim your pen. So I bit off a piece of my pen, then began writing; he looked at me and said: Yes, illuminate it as Allah the Mighty and Majestic illuminated it."
"81","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, that he disliked writing the mushaf in something small."
"82","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Mughirah narrated to us from Ibrahim, that he used to say: Keep the Qur'an unmixed, and do not mix with it what is not of it."
"83","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Awanah narrated to us from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: It was said: Selling and buying the Qur'an and writing it for a wage is disliked; and it was said: The mushaf is not inherited, it belongs to the reciters of the household; and he disliked adorning the mushaf, marking tenths, or making it small. He said: And it was said: Magnify the Qur'an and do not mix with it what is not of it; and he disliked writing it in gold or marking at the ends of verses. He said: And it was said: Keep the Qur'an unmixed."
"84","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he disliked dotting the mushaf."
"85","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Fudayl narrated to us from Layth, from Mujahid, that he disliked diminutives for the mushaf and the mosque, saying musayhif and musayjid."
"86","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Mansur narrated to us, saying: I asked al-Hasan about dotting the masahif, and he said: There is no harm in it, so long as you do not transgress."
"87","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym, a reporter, informed us from Abu Ma'shar, from Ibrahim, who said: Licking the anus is dearer to me than dotting the masahif."
"88","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us from Khalid al-Hadhdha, who said: I entered upon Ibn Sirin and saw him reciting from a dotted mushaf."
"89","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Abu Raja Muhammad ibn Sayf, who said: I asked al-Hasan about a mushaf dotted in Arabic, and he said: There is no harm in it; has it not reached you from Umar's letter that he wrote: Learn Arabic, understand the religion, and interpret dreams well? Abu Raja said: And I asked Ibn Sirin about that, and he said: I fear you will add to the letters."
"90","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us from Shu'bah, from Mansur ibn Zadhdhan, who said: I asked al-Hasan and Ibn Sirin about that, and both said: There is no harm in it."
"91","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Husayn narrated to us, saying: Ubayd Allah ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, saying: I saw Abd Allah ibn Abbas asked about the Arabic of the Qur'an, and he recited poetry."
"92","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym, Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, who said: They disliked interpreting anything of the Qur'an upon occurrences of worldly speech. It was said to Hushaym: Like His saying: {You came at the decreed time, O Musa}? He said: Yes."
"93","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Juwaybir narrated to us from al-Dahhak, who said: Were it not for recitation of the Qur'an, I would love to be a bedridden man until I die; for hardship is lifted from the sick, his sins are expiated, and there is recorded for him the righteous he used to do."
"94","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab, from Abu Abd Allah al-Thaqafi, who said: A man from the people of al-Mada'in narrated to us, saying: I heard Salman al-Farisi say: Whatever Allah the Mighty and Majestic has not mentioned in the Qur'an is of the pardon of Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"95","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Husayn narrated to us from Abd Allah ibn Urwah ibn al-Zubayr, who said: I said to my grandmother Asma: How did the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) act when they recited the Qur'an? She said: They were as Allah the Mighty and Majestic described them: their eyes shed tears and their skins shivered. I said: There are people here who, when they hear that, are seized by fainting. She said: I seek refuge in Allah from Satan."
"96","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Mughirah narrated to us from Ibrahim, who said: The Qur'an is taken away by night, raised from the bellies of men; they wake up neither confirming speech nor confirming women, mounting one another as donkeys mount; then Allah sends a wind that seizes the soul of every believer."
"97","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Rufay' narrated to us, having heard Shaddad ibn Ma'qil, having heard Abd Allah ibn Mas'ud say: The first of your religion you will lose is trustworthiness, and the last that remains is prayer. This Qur'an among you is about to be raised. They said: How, when Allah has established it in our hearts and we have established it in the masahif? He said: It is taken away by night; what is in your hearts goes, and what is in the masahif is raised. Then Abdullah recited: {And if We willed, We would take away what We revealed to you; then you would find no guardian for it against Us}."
"98","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Uthman ibn al-Aswad, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Mujahid, who said: When you yawn while reciting, stop reciting until it leaves you."
"99","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd Allah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us from Abd al-Aziz ibn Abi Rawwad, from Mujahid, who said: He would sometimes recite while people slept, and sense wind, and stop reciting until it passed."
"100","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Zurzur, who said: I heard a man ask Ata, saying: I recite the Qur'an and wind comes from me. He said: Stop reciting until it leaves you."
"101","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sharik narrated to us from Layth, from Ata, Tawus, and Mujahid, that they said: None touches the Qur'an except in purity - or they said: the mushaf."
"102","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated to us from Muslim al-A'war, who said: A man called Abd al-Rahman wrote a mushaf for Mujahid, and he gave him five hundred dirhams."
"103","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Waki' narrated to us from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Ma'qil, that Ubayd Allah ibn Ziyad sent to him to lead people in Ramadan; he led them, and Ubayd Allah sent him a robe and five hundred dirhams, and he said: I do not take a wage for the Qur'an."
"104","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Sa'id ibn Iyas al-Jurayri, from Abd Allah ibn Shaqiq, who said: The Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked selling the masahif and teaching boys for a wage, and deemed it grave."
"105","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Awanah narrated to us from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he disliked the teacher stipulating a wage."
"106","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Ayyub ibn Abi Miskin, from Ata, or Khalid, from Abu Qilabah, that both saw no harm in a wage."
"107","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Fudayl narrated to us from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from al-Hasan, who said: When the teacher bargains and is not just, he is recorded among the oppressors."
"108","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Safwan ibn Amr, from Umayr ibn Hani, that a man used to teach a man the Qur'an; that man performed Hajj and gifted the one who taught him a bow. He came to Awf ibn Malik and told him, and he said to him: Cast it off. He said: I intend to fight with it. He said: Cast it off. He said: I intend to fight with it. Awf said to him: Do you wish to hang a bow of fire? The man returned it to its owner."
"109","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us from Abd Rabbihi ibn Sulayman ibn Zaytun, from al-Tufayl ibn Amr, who said: Ubayy taught me the Qur'an, and I gifted him a bow. He went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wearing it, and he said: Who armed you with this? He said: Al-Tufayl ibn Amr; I taught him the Qur'an. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: You would wear it as a strip of the fire of Hell. They said: O Messenger of Allah, we eat of their food. He said: As for food made for other than you which you attend, there is no harm in eating it; as for what is made for you, you eat only by your share (of reward)."
"110","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Abu Husayn, from Abu al-Duha, who said: I asked three, sparing none: Abd Allah ibn Yazid, Masruq, and Shurayh, about selling the masahif, and they said: Do not take a price for the Book of Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"111","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, saying: I asked Muhammad ibn Sirin about writers of the masahif for a wage, and he said: Writing them, commissioning their writing, selling them, and buying them were disliked."
"112","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Awanah narrated to us from Abu Husayn, from Abu al-Duha, who said: I asked Shurayh, Masruq, and Abd Allah ibn Yazid about selling the masahif, and they said: Do not take a price for the Book of Allah."
"113","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad al-Ammi narrated to us, saying: Malik ibn Dinar narrated to us, saying: Jabir ibn Zayd came to me while I was writing, and I said: How do you see this craft of mine, O Abu al-Sha'tha? He said: How fine your craft is: you transfer the Book of Allah from sheet to sheet, verse to verse, word to word; this is lawful, there is no harm in it."
"114","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Jarir narrated to us from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Alqamah wanted to write a mushaf and disliked paying a wage for its writing; so he bought its paper, ink, and needs, and gave it to one of his companions, who wrote it for him."
"115","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abd al-Aziz ibn Abd al-Samad narrated to us, saying: Malik ibn Dinar narrated to us that Ikrimah sold a mushaf of his, and that al-Hasan saw no harm in it."
"116","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Yunus narrated to us from al-Hasan, that he saw no harm in selling and buying them."
"117","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Dawud narrated to us from al-Sha'bi, that he was asked about that and said: He sells only the price of its paper and the wage of its scribe."
"118","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us from Dawud, from al-Sha'bi, the like thereof."
"119","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Layth narrated to us from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, who said: Buy the masahif but do not sell them."
"120","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us from Layth, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him - the like thereof."
"121","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Abu Bishr informed us from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: Buy it but do not sell it."
"122","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Awanah narrated to us from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: Buy the mushaf but do not sell it."
"123","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hushaym narrated to us, saying: Khalid narrated to us from Ibn Sirin, from Ubaydah al-Salmani, that he disliked selling and buying the masahif."
"124","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Salim ibn Abd Allah, who said: Ibn Umar said: I wish hands were cut off for selling the masahif."
"125","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, saying: I asked Muhammad ibn Sirin about the writing of the teacher, and he said: There was a teacher in Medina, and with him were the children of those notables; he was a slave, and his masters imposed things upon him; the boys would say: Let us suffice you - but he refused them."
"126","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, saying: Abu Ishaq narrated to us from Abd Allah ibn Habib, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The Mufassal was revealed in Makkah; we stayed years reciting it, nothing else coming down."
"127","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us from Abu Ishaq, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Let not one who recites the Qur'an deceive you; it is but speech spoken. Rather look at one who acts by it."
"128","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, saying: Abu Malik al-Ashja'i narrated to us from Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal al-Ashja'i, from his father, who said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I am newly out of idolatry, so command me with something that clears me of idolatry. He said: Recite: {Say: O disbelievers}. His father never missed it day or night until he left the world."
"129","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us from Abu al-Hasan al-Taymi, who said: I heard a man say: I was traveling with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a dark night, and he heard a reciter reciting: {Say: O disbelievers}; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for this one, he is cleared of idolatry. We traveled on, and he heard a man reciting: {Say: He is Allah, One}; he said: As for this one, he is forgiven. So I held back my mount to see who he was, to give him glad tidings; I looked right and left but saw no one."
"130","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us from al-Awza'i, from Isma'il ibn Ubayd Allah, from a freedman of Fadalah ibn Ubayd, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah listens more attentively to a man with a beautiful voice reciting the Qur'an than the owner of a singing girl listens to his singing girl."
"131","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard the recitation of Abu Musa and said: This one has been given of the flutes of the family of Dawud."
"132","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I entered Paradise and heard a recitation; I said: Who is this? They said: Harithah ibn al-Nu'man. Such is righteousness; such is righteousness."
"133","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Sufyan narrated to us from Ammar al-Duhni, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, that Ali assigned - or gave - to whoever recited the Qur'an two thousand each; and my father was among those who recited the Qur'an but did not take."
"134","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abi Nadrah narrated to us from Sa'id ibn Iyas al-Jurayri, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Firas, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O people, there came upon me a time when I did not know that anyone intended by his recitation other than Allah the Mighty and Majestic, until it later appeared to me that peoples intend by their recitation other than Allah; so intend Allah the Mighty and Majestic by your recitation and your deeds."
"135","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu Shihab narrated to us from al-Salt ibn Bahram, from al-Hasan, who said: This Qur'an has been recited by slaves and boys who did not take it from its beginning nor have knowledge of its interpretation. The most deserving of people of this Qur'an is he who is seen in his deed. Allah the Blessed and Exalted said: {A Book We sent down to you, blessed, that they ponder its verses and that men of understanding remember}. Pondering its verses is but following it by deed. One of them says to his companion: Come, let me recite against you. By Allah, the reciters did not do this. By Allah, they are not reciters nor pious. May Allah not multiply their likes among people; may Allah not multiply their likes among people."
"136","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us from Abu Sinan, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, or from Abu Hurayrah, who said: Whoever recites one hundred verses in a night is recorded among the devout, and whoever maintains the five prayers is not recorded among the heedless."
"137","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us from Abu Sinan, from Ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, who said: They disliked reciting part of a verse and leaving part."
"138","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us from Abu Sinan, from al-Mughirah ibn Subay', who said: Whoever recites at his sleep verses of al-Baqarah does not forget the Qur'an: four verses from: {And your God is One God; there is no god but He, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate}; and Ayat al-Kursi; and the last three verses of it."
"139","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, who said: Abdullah said: It is not a mistake to make the ending of one verse the ending of another verse."
"140","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us from Ayyub, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, who said: Let none of you say: I have taken the whole Qur'an; what does he know what all of it is? Much Qur'an has gone from it. Rather let him say: We have taken what is apparent of it."
"141","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us from Abu Sinan, from Abd Allah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Hanzalah ibn Khuwaylid al-Anazi, who said: I went out with Ibn Mas'ud until he came to the bench - the bench of the market - faced it, then said: O Allah, I ask You of its good and the good of its people, and I seek refuge in You from its evil and the evil of its people. Then he walked until he came to the steps of the mosque, and heard a man swearing by a surah of the Qur'an, and said: O Hanzalah, do you think this one expiates his oath? For every verse there is an expiation - or he said: an oath."
"142","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us from al-A'mash, from Abd Allah ibn Murrah, from Abu Kanaf, who said: While I was walking with Ibn Mas'ud in the slave market, he heard a man swearing by a surah of the Qur'an, and Ibn Mas'ud said: Upon him for every verse of it is an oath."
"143","Al-A'mash said: I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: Abdullah said: Whoever swears by the Qur'an, upon him for every verse is an oath; and whoever disbelieves in a verse of the Qur'an has disbelieved in all of it."
"144","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us from Ghaylan, from Mutarrif, who said: Let none of you say: Allah the Mighty and Majestic says so-and-so; rather say: Allah the Mighty and Majestic said."
"145","Sa'id narrated to us, saying: Mus'ab ibn Mahan narrated to us from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Ibrahim, that a man used to write the Qur'an and water it (erase with saliva), and he said: I see a trial will befall him. [Flag: source wording terse; translated literally.]"
"146","146 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, from Abu al-Ahwas, who said: 'Abdullah said: Recite the Qur'an in seven (days), and do not recite it in fewer than three, and let a man maintain his portion in his day and his night."
"147","147 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu 'Ubaydah, who said: 'Abdullah said: Whoever recites the Qur'an in fewer than three (days) is rajiz (glib and hasty), reciting hastily like the hasty recitation of poetry, and scattering (words) like the scattering of inferior dates."
"148","148 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: Whoever recites the Qur'an in fewer than three (days) is rajiz (glib and hasty)."
"149","149 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from Husayn, from 'Ubaydullah ibn 'Abdullah, who said: Ibn Mas'ud used to complete the Qur'an in three (days), seeking help for it from the daytime only a little."
"150","150 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, from Muhammad ibn Dhakwan, who said: I heard 'Abd al-Rahman ibn 'Abdullah, from 'Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, that he used to complete the Qur'an in Ramadan in three (days), and outside Ramadan from Friday to Friday."
"151","151 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Fudayl ibn 'Iyad narrated, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, who said: Al-Aswad used to complete the Qur'an in the month of Ramadan every two nights, and he would sleep between Maghrib and 'Isha, and he used to complete it outside of that in six (days)."
"152","152 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Fudayl ibn 'Iyad narrated, from Sulayman, from Ibrahim, who said: 'Alqamah used to complete the Qur'an every five (days), and al-Aswad used to complete it every six, and 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid used to complete it every seven."
"153","153 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated, from Abu 'Ubaydah, from his father, who said: Whoever recites more than a third of the Qur'an in one night is rajiz (glib and hasty)."
"154","154 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Khalid narrated, from Abu Qilabah, that Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to complete the Qur'an every eight (days), and that Tamim al-Dari used to complete it every seven."
"155","155 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Abu al-Muhallab, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, that he used to complete the Qur'an every eight (days)."
"156","156 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Sayyar narrated, from Abu Wa'il, from 'Abdullah, who said: A man came to him and said: I recited al-Mufassal last night in one rak'ah, so he became angry and said: It was only divided into separate sections so that you recite it section by section; (do not recite) hastily like the hasty recitation of poetry, and scattering (words) like the scattering of inferior dates. I know the pairs (of surahs) that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to join together, two surahs in each rak'ah, two surahs in each rak'ah."
"157","157 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Nafi' ibn Labibah, who said: I said to Ibn 'Umar: I recited al-Mufassal in one rak'ah, so he said: Did you (really) do it? If Allah Almighty had willed to send it down as a single bulk He would have done so; give every surah its share of bowing and prostration."
"158","158 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from Ibn Sirin, who said: The wife of 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said when he was killed: You have killed him, and indeed he would keep the whole night alive with the Qur'an in one rak'ah."
"159","159 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Shihab narrated, from al-'Ala ibn al-Musayyab, from Talut, from Ibn 'Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: That I recite al-Baqarah in one night is dearer to me than reciting the whole Qur'an in one night."
"160","160 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Isma'il ibn Muslim al-'Abdi, from Abu al-Mutawakkil al-Naji, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood one night, and spent his night with one verse of the Qur'an repeating it to himself."
"161","161 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, from Abu Jamrah, who said: I said to Ibn 'Abbas: I recite the Qur'an in one night once, or twice - he said: my strongest recollection is that he said: twice - so Ibn 'Abbas said: That I recite nothing but one surah is dearer to me than doing that; and if you must do it, then recite a recitation that your ears hear and your heart retains."
"162","162 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, from 'Abd Rabbihi and Yahya the two sons of Sa'id, from a Tabban man of the people of Madinah, that he heard his father say: I heard Zayd ibn Thabit being asked about the recitation of the Qur'an; he said: That I recite in a month is dearer to me than fifteen (days), and fifteen is dearer to me than ten, and ten is dearer to me than seven; I pause where one ought to pause, and I supplicate Allah Almighty and ask Him."
"163","163 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated, from Muhammad ibn Yazid, from 'Umayr ibn Rabi'ah, who said: I saw Abu al-Darda studying the Qur'an among a group of his companions."
"164","164 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, who said: 'Abdullah was brought a mushaf that had been adorned, so he said: The finest adornment with which the mushaf is adorned is its truthful recitation."
"165","165 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Faraj ibn Faddalah narrated, from Abu Sa'id al-Ansari, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: When you ornament your masahif and decorate your mosques, then destruction is upon you."
"166","166 - Sa'id narrated, he said: al-Harith ibn 'Ubayd al-Iyadi narrated, from Abu 'Imran al-Jawni, from Jundub ibn 'Abdullah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Recite the Qur'an as long as your hearts are united upon it, but when you disagree then stand up (and stop)."
"167","167 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This Qur'an has a period of eagerness, then people have a period of lull away from it; so whoever's lull is toward moderation, then blessed is he, and whoever's lull is toward turning away, then those are ruined."
"168","Book of Tafsir. Chapter of the commentary of the Opening of the Book. 168 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abdullah ibn Ja'far narrated, from al-'Ala ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ya'qub, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever prays a prayer in which he does not recite the Opening of the Book (Umm al-Qur'an), it is incomplete, it is incomplete, not complete. I said to him: O Abu Hurayrah, sometimes I am behind an imam? So he squeezed my arm and said: O Persian, recite it to yourself, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah Almighty said: I have divided the surah between Me and My servant into two halves; half of it is for Me and half of it is for My servant. The servant says: 'Praise be to Allah, Lord of the worlds,' so Allah says: My servant has praised Me; and the servant says: 'The Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate,' so Allah says: My servant has extolled Me; and the servant says: 'Master of the Day of Judgment,' so Allah Almighty says: My servant has glorified Me - this is for Me, and for My servant is what he asks - and this verse is between Me and My servant. The servant says: 'You alone we worship and You alone we ask for help' - and this is between Me and My servant in halves, and for My servant is what he asks. The servant says: 'Guide us along the Straight Path, the path of those upon whom You have bestowed favor, not of those who incurred wrath nor of those who went astray.'"
"169","169 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Mukhbir narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Abu Bakr, 'Umar and 'Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them, used to recite: 'Master (Malik) of the Day of Judgment.'"
"170","170 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from al-Hajjaj, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, that he heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, recite likewise."
"171","171 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Khalid narrated, from Abu Qilabah, that Ubayy ibn Ka'b used to recite: 'Master (Malik) of the Day of Judgment.'"
"172","172 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated, he said: al-A'mash narrated, from Ibrahim, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to recite: 'Master (Malik) of the Day of Judgment,' and 'Alqamah and al-Aswad used to recite: 'Master (Malik) of the Day of Judgment.'"
"173","173 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Yahya ibn Waththab, that he used to recite: 'Master (Malik) of the Day of Judgment.'"
"174","Concerning His saying: 'Guide us along the Straight Path' - 174 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Suwayd ibn 'Abd al-'Aziz narrated, he said: Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated, he said: Murrah al-Hamdani narrated to me, from Ibn Mas'ud, who said: The path (sirat) is over the Fire; the first of them cross like lightning, then like birds, then like excellent horses, and the last of them crosses crawling; and the angels are standing with them, with hooks of fire, snatching people right and left until they hurl them into the Fire."
"175","175 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from Thabit, who heard Ibn 'Abbas recite: 'as-sirat' with a sin."
"176","Concerning His saying: 'the path of those upon whom You have bestowed favor' - 176 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Muhammad ibn 'Amr ibn 'Alqamah, from Yahya ibn 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father, that he heard 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, recite: '(the path of those upon whom You have bestowed favor)'."
"177","Concerning His saying: 'not of those who incurred wrath nor of those who went astray' - 177 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from al-Aswad, that 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to recite: '(not of those who incurred wrath nor of those other than the ones gone astray)'."
"178","178 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sallam al-Tawil narrated, from Zayd al-'Ammi, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Opening of the Book is a cure for poison."
"179","179 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to 'Adi ibn Hatim: Those who incurred wrath are the Jews, and the Christians are those gone astray."
"180","Chapter of the commentary of Surat al-Baqarah. Concerning His saying: 'Alif Lam Mim. That is the Book in which there is no doubt, a guidance for the God-conscious, who believe in the unseen' - 180 - Sa'id ibn Mansur narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from 'Umarah ibn 'Umayr, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from 'Abdullah, who said: The companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and their faith were mentioned, so 'Abdullah said: The affair of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was clear to whoever saw him; by Him besides whom there is no god, no believer has believed with a belief better than belief in the unseen; then he recited: 'Alif Lam Mim. That is the Book in which there is no doubt, a guidance for the God-conscious, who believe in the unseen.'"
"181","181 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, he said: al-Harith ibn Qays said to 'Abdullah: With Allah we reckon what you preceded us in, O companions of Muhammad, of seeing the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so 'Abdullah said: We reckon your faith in Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) though you did not see him."
"182","Concerning His saying: 'Allah has set a seal upon their hearts and upon their hearing, and over their eyes is a covering' - 182 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Abu al-Ashhab narrated, from al-Hasan and Abu Raja; one of them recited: 'a covering (ghishawah),' and the other: 'a covering (ghashwah).'"
"183","Concerning His saying: 'The lightning almost snatches away their sight' - 183 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: 'Abbad ibn Rashid narrated, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: 'The lightning almost snatches away their sight.'"
"184","Concerning His saying: 'They said: Will You place therein one who will cause corruption therein and shed blood, while we declare Your praise and sanctify You? He said: I know what you do not know' - 184 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih or another, from Mujahid, concerning His Almighty saying: 'I know what you do not know,' he said: He knew of the disobedience of Iblis and created him for it."
"185","Concerning His saying: 'But when He informed them of their names He said: Did I not tell you that I know the unseen of the heavens and the earth, and I know what you reveal and what you used to conceal' - 185 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated, he said: We were with al-Hasan, and al-Hasan ibn Dinar asked him, saying: O Abu Sa'id, what do you say about Allah Almighty's saying to the angels: 'and I know what you reveal and what you used to conceal' - what is it that the angels concealed? He said: When Allah created Adam, the angels saw a wondrous creation, so as if something of that entered them; then some of them turned to others and concealed that among themselves, saying: What concern to you is the affair of this creature? Allah does not create any creature but that we are more honored to Him than it."
"186","Concerning His saying: 'Then Adam received words from his Lord, so He turned to him; indeed He is the Oft-Returning, the Merciful' - 186 - Sa'id narrated, he said: al-Hasan ibn Yazid al-Asamm narrated, he said: I heard al-Suddi say concerning His Almighty saying: 'Then Adam received words from his Lord, so He turned to him; indeed He is the Oft-Returning, the Merciful,' he said: My Lord, You created me with Your hand and breathed into me of Your spirit, and Your mercy preceded Your anger; if I repent and amend, will You return me to Paradise? He said: It was said: Yes."
"187","Concerning His saying: 'And when We said to the angels: Prostrate to Adam, so they prostrated except Iblis; he refused and was arrogant and was of the disbelievers' - 187 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When the son of Adam recites the prostration (verse) and prostrates, Satan withdraws weeping and says: O woe to him; the son of Adam was commanded to prostrate and he prostrated, so for him is Paradise; and I was commanded to prostrate and I refused, so for me is the Fire."
"188","Concerning His saying: 'And We said: O Adam, dwell you and your wife in Paradise, and eat therefrom freely wherever you will, but do not approach this tree, lest you be of the wrongdoers. Then Satan caused them to slip therefrom and brought them out of what they were in' - 188 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from Bayan, from 'Amir al-Sha'bi, from Ja'dah ibn Hubayrah, who said: The tree by which Adam was tested is the grapevine, and it was made a trial for his offspring after him."
"189","Concerning His saying: 'And seek help through patience and prayer; and indeed it is great except for the humble' - 189 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Safwan narrated, from Zayd ibn 'Ali, from Ibn 'Abbas, that while he was on a journey of his, a son of his was announced dead to him, so he dismounted and prayed two rak'ahs, then said the istirja' (we belong to Allah and to Him we return), and said: We did as Allah the Exalted commanded us: 'And seek help through patience and prayer.'"
"190","Concerning His saying: 'Then ask your Lord to bring forth for us of what the earth grows, its herbs and its cucumbers and its fum and its lentils and its onions' - 190 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from Husayn, from Abu Malik, concerning His Almighty saying: 'and its fum,' he said: meaning wheat."
"191","191 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated - and he was asked about it - and said: as 'Abdullah recites it: '(and its thum)' (garlic)."
"192","Concerning His saying: 'He said: It is a yellow cow, bright in its color, pleasing to the beholders' - 192 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Nuh ibn Qays narrated, from Muhammad ibn Sayf, from al-Hasan, concerning His Almighty saying: 'yellow, bright in its color,' he said: It is black, intensely black."
"193","Concerning His saying: 'They said: Call upon your Lord for us to make clear to us what it is; indeed cows look alike to us; and indeed we, if Allah wills, will be guided' - 193 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ikrimah, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Had the Children of Israel taken the lowest cow and slaughtered it, it would have sufficed them; but they made matters strict; and had they not said: 'if Allah wills, we will be guided,' they would not have found it."
"194","Concerning His saying: 'And speak to people good' - 194 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from 'Ata, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And speak to people good,' he said: To all people, to the associater (mushrik) and to other than the associater."
"195","195 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit used to recite: 'And speak to people good (husna),' and Ibn Mas'ud used to recite: 'And speak to people good (husna).'"
"196","Concerning His saying: 'And if they come to you as captives, you ransom them' - 196 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he used to recite: 'And if they come to you as captives (asra).'"
"197","197 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Mughirah narrated, from Ibrahim, that he used to recite: 'And if they come to you as captives (asra), you ransom them (tafduhum).'"
"198","198 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Humayd, that he used to recite: 'captives (asra).'"
"199","199 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: 'Abbad ibn Rashid narrated, from al-Hasan, that he used to recite: 'captives (usara), you ransom them (tufaduhum).'"
"200","Concerning His saying: 'And We supported him with the Holy Spirit' - 200 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Amr ibn Thabit al-Haddad narrated, from his father, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And We supported him with the Holy Spirit,' he said: He used to give life to the dead."
"201","Concerning His saying: 'And of those who associated, one of them wishes he could be granted a life of a thousand years, but it would not remove him from the punishment that he be granted life' - 201 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn 'Abbas, concerning His Almighty saying: 'one of them wishes he could be granted a life of a thousand years,' he said: It is the saying of the non-Arabs when one of them sneezes, it is said to him: 'zih hizar sal' - meaning a thousand years."
"202","Concerning His saying: 'Whoever is an enemy to Allah and His angels and His messengers and Gabriel and Michael, then indeed Allah is an enemy to the disbelievers' - 202 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Yahya ibn Waththab, that he used to recite: 'and Gabriel (Jibril) and Michael (Mikayil).'"
"203","Concerning His saying: 'And they followed what the devils recited over the kingdom of Solomon; and Solomon did not disbelieve, but the devils disbelieved, teaching people magic and what was sent down upon the two angels at Babylon, Harut and Marut; and they do not teach anyone until they say: We are only a trial, so do not disbelieve; so they learn from them that by which they separate a man from his wife; and they do not harm anyone thereby except by Allah's permission' - 203 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from 'Amr, from 'Ata, who said: I asked Ibn 'Abbas: How do we recite: 'and they followed (wattaba'u)' or '(ittaba'u)'? He said: They are the same; recite your first recitation."
"204","204 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Attab ibn Bashir narrated, he said: Khusayf narrated, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And they followed what the devils recited,' he said: When a tree sprouted, Solomon would say: For which ailment are you? And it would say: For such and such; then when the Syrian carob tree sprouted, he said: For what are you? It said: For your mosque - I shall ruin it. He said: You shall ruin it?! It said: Yes. He said: What an evil tree you are! Then he did not remain long before he died. So people began to say regarding their sick: If only we had the like of Solomon! Then they took to the devils, and they took a book and placed it in Solomon's place of prayer, saying: We shall guide you to what Solomon used to treat with. So they went and brought out that book, and in it was magic and incantations. So Allah Almighty sent down: '(And they followed what the devils recited over the kingdom of Solomon; and Solomon did not disbelieve, but the devils are the ones who disbelieved, teaching people magic and what was sent down upon the two angels).' And he mentioned that in the recitation of Ubayy it is: '(and what is recited unto the two angels at Babylon, Harut and Marut; and they do not teach anyone until they say: We are only a trial, so do not disbelieve)' - seven times - then if he refuses but to disbelieve, they teach him; so there comes out of him a fire - or a light - until it shines into the sky. He said: The knowledge that he used to know."
"205","205 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Attab ibn Bashir narrated, from Khusayf, who said: I was with Mujahid, when a man of Quraysh passed by us, so Mujahid said to him: Narrate to us what you heard from your father. He said: My father narrated to me that when the angels began to look at the deeds of the children of Adam and the foul sins they commit - and nothing conceals people from the angels - some of them began to say to others: Look at the children of Adam, how they do such and such; how bold they are against Allah - faulting them thereby. So Allah Almighty said to them: I have heard what you say about the children of Adam; so choose two angels from among you whom I shall send down to the earth, and I shall place in them the desire of the children of Adam. So they chose Harut and Marut, saying: O Lord, there is none among us like them. So they were sent down to the earth, and He placed in them the desire of the children of Adam, and al-Zuhrah (Venus) was presented to them in the form of a woman; when they looked at her they could not restrain themselves from taking from her what Allah knows best, and desire seized their hearing and their sight. When they wanted to fly to the sky they could not. Then an angel came to them and said: You have done what you have done, so choose the punishment of this world or the punishment of the Hereafter. One of them said to the other: What do you see? He said: I see that being punished in this world then being punished (again) is dearer to me than being punished a single hour in the Hereafter. So they are suspended upside down in chains, and they were made a trial."
"206","206 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Shihab ibn Khirash narrated, from al-'Awwam ibn Hawshab, from Mujahid, who said: I was with Ibn 'Umar - I think he said: on a journey - when he said to me: Watch the bright star (al-kawkabah); when it rises, wake me. When it rose I woke him; he sat upright and began to look at it and curse it severely. I said: May Allah have mercy on you, O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, a hearing, obedient star - why is it cursed? He said: Ha! This was a prostitute among the Children of Israel, and the two angels met from her what they met."
"207","207 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from Husayn ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from 'Imran ibn al-Harith al-Sulami, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A man came to him and said to him: From where have you come? He said: From Iraq. He said: How did you leave the people behind you? He said: I left the people talking that 'Ali will come out to them. He said: Had we known, we would not have married his women nor divided his inheritance. And I shall narrate to you about that: the devils used to eavesdrop in the sky, and when one of them heard a word of truth, he lied along with it a thousand lies; so the hearts of people absorbed them and took them as registers. Solomon came upon them and buried them beneath his chair. When Solomon died, devils stood by the road saying: Shall we not guide you to the protected treasure of Solomon, which has no treasure like it? So they brought them out. They said: It is magic; and the remainder of it is what the people of Iraq talk about. And Allah sent down the vindication of Solomon regarding what they said of magic: 'And they followed what the devils recited over the kingdom of Solomon,' to the end of the verse."
"208","Concerning His saying: 'Whatever verse We abrogate or cause to be forgotten, We bring one better than it or like it' - 208 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Ya'la ibn 'Ata narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Rabi'ah ibn Qanif al-Thaqafi, who said: I heard Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas recite: '(Whatever verse We abrogate or We forget),' so I said to him: Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab recites: '(Whatever verse We abrogate or cause to be forgotten).' He said: The Qur'an was not sent down upon al-Musayyab, nor upon the family of al-Musayyab. Allah the Exalted said: 'We shall make you recite, so you shall not forget,' and: 'And remember your Lord when you forget.'"
"209","209 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman, from 'Ata, concerning His Almighty saying: '(Whatever verse We abrogate or cause to be forgotten),' he said: Or We delay it."
"210","Concerning His saying: 'And to Allah belong the east and the west; so wherever you turn, there is the face of Allah; indeed Allah is All-Encompassing, All-Knowing' - 210 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn 'Ayyash narrated, he said: Hajjaj narrated to me, from 'Ata, that a people for whom the qiblah was obscured, so each person of them prayed toward a direction; then they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and mentioned that to him, so Allah sent down upon His Messenger: 'So wherever you turn, there is the face of Allah.'"
"211","Concerning His saying: 'Those to whom We gave the Book recite it with its true recitation; those believe in it' - 211 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Attab ibn Bashir narrated, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: 'recite it with its true recitation,' he said: They act upon it with its true action; 'those believe in it.'"
"212","Concerning His saying: 'My covenant does not reach the wrongdoers' - 212 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Attab narrated, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, concerning His Almighty saying: 'My covenant does not reach the wrongdoers,' he said: There will be among your offspring a wrongdoer."
"213","213 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His Almighty saying: 'My covenant does not reach the wrongdoers,' he said: If he is a wrongdoer, he is not an imam to be followed."
"214","Concerning His saying: 'And take the station of Abraham as a place of prayer' - 214 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih and others, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: 'And take the station of Abraham as a place of prayer,' (he said:) a place of supplication (mudda'an)."
"215","215 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Humayd al-Tawil narrated, he said: Anas ibn Malik narrated, he said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I agreed with my Lord in three: I said: O Messenger of Allah, if only you took the station of Abraham as a place of prayer; so there was sent down: 'And take the station of Abraham as a place of prayer.' And I said: O Messenger of Allah, the righteous and the wicked enter upon your wives; if only you commanded them to veil themselves; so the verse of the veil was sent down. And the wives of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered, so I said to them: 'It may be that his Lord, if he divorces you, will give him in exchange wives better than you, Muslim' - the verse - so it was sent down."
"216","216 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated, from Abu Ja'far the freedman of Ibn 'Ayyash, that he used to recite: 'And take the station of Abraham as a place of prayer.'"
"217","Concerning His saying: 'And We entrusted Abraham and Ishmael to purify My House for those who circumambulate and those who stay and those who bow and prostrate' - 217 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from 'Ata, concerning His saying: 'Purify My House for those who circumambulate,' he said: There were idols in it, so they were commanded to remove them from it."
"218","Concerning His saying: 'And when Abraham said: My Lord, make this a secure land and provide its people with fruits, whoever of them believes in Allah and the Last Day. He said: And whoever disbelieves, I shall grant him enjoyment a little, then compel him to the punishment of the Fire, and evil is the destination' - 218 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, who said: Abraham asked Allah Almighty for provision for the people of the land who believed; he said: 'And provide its people with fruits, whoever of them believes in Allah and the Last Day.' Allah Almighty said: 'And whoever disbelieves' - I shall provide him too; I shall grant him enjoyment a little, then compel him to the punishment of the Fire."
"219","Concerning His saying: 'And when Abraham raised the foundations of the House, and Ishmael: Our Lord, accept from us; indeed You are the All-Hearing, the All-Knowing' - 219 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, and recited this verse: 'And when Abraham raised the foundations of the House, and Ishmael: Our Lord, accept from us.' He said: They asked for acceptance, fearing that something from them might not be accepted."
"220","Concerning His saying: 'Our Lord, make us Muslims to You, and of our offspring a Muslim nation to You, and show us our rites, and turn to us; indeed You are the Oft-Returning, the Merciful' - 220 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Attab narrated: Khusayf informed us, from Mujahid, who said: Abraham said: Our Lord, show us our rites. So Gabriel, peace be upon him, took his hand and went with him until he brought him to the House; he said: Raise the foundations. So Abraham raised the foundations and completed the building. Then he took him to al-Safa and said: This is of the symbols of Allah. Then he took him to al-Marwah and said: And this is of the symbols of Allah. Then he took his hand and went with him toward Mina, when Iblis appeared at al-'Aqabah by the tree. Gabriel said to him: Say takbir and pelt him! So he said takbir and pelted, and Iblis went until he stood at the middle jamrah; Gabriel and Abraham faced him, and Gabriel said: Say takbir and pelt him! So he said takbir and pelted, and Iblis went until he came to the farthest jamrah; Gabriel said to him: Say takbir and pelt him! So he said takbir and pelted, and Iblis went. The wicked one had wanted to introduce something into the Hajj but could not. Then he went until he brought him to al-Mash'ar al-Haram, saying: This is al-Mash'ar al-Haram. Then he went until he brought him to 'Arafat, saying: These are 'Arafat; have you recognized what I showed you? He said: Yes - three times. He said: Then proclaim the Hajj among the people. He said: And how shall I proclaim? He said: Say: O people, answer your Lord - three times. So the servants answered: 'Labbayk, O Allah, our Lord, Labbayk' - twice. So whoever of creation answered Abraham that day is a pilgrim. Mujahid said to me: O Abu 'Awn, the Qadariyyah do not believe in this."
"221","Concerning His saying: 'Say: We believe in Allah and what was sent down to us and what was sent down to Abraham and Ishmael and Isaac and Jacob and the tribes, and what was given to Moses and Jesus, and what was given to the prophets from their Lord; we make no distinction between any of them, and to Him we are Muslims' - 221 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Juwaybir narrated, from al-Dahhak, who said: Teach your children, your families and your servants the names of the prophets whom Allah mentioned in His Book, so that they believe in them and affirm what they brought; for Allah says: 'Say: We believe in Allah and what was sent down to us and what was sent down to Abraham and Ishmael and Isaac and Jacob and the tribes, and what was given to Moses and Jesus, and what was given to the prophets from their Lord; we make no distinction between any of them, and to Him we are Muslims.'"
"222","Concerning His saying: 'And thus We made you a middle nation, that you be witnesses over people and the Messenger be a witness over you' - 222 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, he said: al-A'mash narrated, from Abu Salih, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), concerning His saying: 'And thus We made you a middle nation,' he said: Just; 'that you be witnesses over people,' he said: A prophet will be brought on the Day of Resurrection with him a single man who followed him and none other, and a prophet with him two men whom no more than that followed; so it will be said to the prophet: Did you convey to these? He will say: Yes. Then it will be said to them: Did he convey to you? They will say: No. Then it will be said to them: Who testifies for you that you conveyed? They will say: Muhammad and his nation. So they will testify for them of the conveying. Then it will be said to them: How do you know? They will say: Our Prophet informed us that the messengers conveyed, so we believed that. That is His Almighty saying: 'We made you a middle nation' - meaning: just - 'that you be witnesses over people' - he said: over these nations that they were conveyed to."
"223","Concerning His saying: 'And Allah would never waste your faith; indeed Allah is to people Most Kind, Most Merciful. We see the turning of your face toward the sky, so We shall surely turn you to a qiblah you are pleased with; so turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque; and wherever you are, turn your faces toward it' - 223 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated: Abu Ishaq al-Hamdani narrated, from al-Bara ibn 'Azib, who said: I prayed with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sixteen months, until the verse that is about the qiblah was sent down: 'And wherever you are, turn your faces toward it.' It was sent down after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had prayed; so a man of the people went and passed by some people of the Ansar while they were praying, and told them the report, so they turned their faces toward the House."
"224","224 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed toward Bayt al-Maqdis sixteen months, then he was commanded to pray toward the Ka'bah. So a man went out and saw some people of the Ansar praying toward Bayt al-Maqdis, and said: I testify that I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) praying toward the Ka'bah; so they all turned toward the Ka'bah."
"225","225 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sharik narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Bara, concerning His saying: 'And Allah would never waste your faith,' he said: Your prayer toward Bayt al-Maqdis."
"226","226 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from Ya'la ibn 'Ata, from Yahya ibn Qamtah, who said: I saw 'Abdullah ibn 'Amr looking at the Ka'bah from the side of the spout (mizab), saying: This is the one about which Allah said: 'We shall surely turn you to a qiblah you are pleased with.'"
"227","227 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from 'Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu al-'Aliyah, concerning His Almighty saying: 'So turn your face toward the Sacred Mosque,' he said: Facing the Sacred Mosque."
"228","Concerning His saying: 'And for each is a direction to which he turns; so race to good deeds; wherever you are, Allah will bring you all together; indeed Allah is over all things Powerful' - 228 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated, from Abu Sinan, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, concerning His saying: 'And for each is a direction to which he turns,' he said: For every people of a religion is a qiblah toward which they pray; 'so race to good deeds' - He says to this nation: 'wherever you are, Allah will bring you all together' - he said: The righteous and the wicked."
"229","Concerning His saying: 'So remember Me, I shall remember you; and be grateful to Me and do not deny Me' - 229 - Sa'id narrated, he said: I heard Fudayl say concerning His saying: 'So remember Me, I shall remember you,' he said: Remember Me through obeying Me, I shall remember you through My forgiveness."
"230","230 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, from Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub, from Abu Hani al-Khawlani, from Khalid ibn Abi 'Imran, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever obeys Allah has remembered Allah, even if his prayer, fasting and recitation of the Qur'an be little; and whoever disobeys Allah has forgotten Allah, even if his prayer, fasting and recitation of the Qur'an be much."
"231","Concerning His saying: 'O you who believed, seek help through patience and prayer; indeed Allah is with the patient' - 231 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated: 'Uyaynah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman informed us, from his father, from Ibn 'Abbas, that his brother Qutham was announced dead to him while he was on a journey; so he said the istirja', then moved aside from the road and prayed two rak'ahs in which he lengthened the sitting; then he stood walking to his mount saying: 'And seek help through patience and prayer; and indeed it is great except for the humble.'"
"232","232 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated: Khalid ibn Safwan narrated, he said: Zayd ibn 'Ali narrated to me, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: A son of his was announced dead to him while he was traveling on a journey; so he dismounted and prayed two rak'ahs, then said the istirja', then said: We did as Allah Almighty commanded us; then he recited this verse: 'Seek help through patience and prayer.'"
"233","Concerning His saying: 'Those - upon them are blessings from their Lord and mercy, and those are the guided' - 233 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Mansur, from Mujahid, who said: 'Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: What fine two equivalents (camels carrying provisions) and what fine an extra load: 'Those - upon them are blessings from their Lord and mercy, and those are the guided.'"
"234","Concerning His saying: 'Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are of the symbols of Allah; so whoever makes Hajj to the House or 'Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate between them; and whoever volunteers good, then indeed Allah is Appreciative, All-Knowing' - 234 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Dawud narrated, from al-Sha'bi, who said: Upon al-Safa was an idol called Isaf, and upon al-Marwah an idol called Na'ilah. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, they said: O Messenger of Allah, the people of the Jahiliyyah would only run between al-Safa and al-Marwah for the two idols upon them, and they are not of the symbols of Allah. So there was sent down: 'Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are of the symbols of Allah; so whoever makes Hajj to the House or 'Umrah, there is no blame upon him to circumambulate between them.'"
"235","235 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated: Ibn Abi Najih narrated, from Mujahid, concerning His Almighty saying: 'Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are of the symbols of Allah,' he said: The Ansar said: Running between these two stones is of the affair of the Jahiliyyah; so there was sent down: 'Indeed al-Safa and al-Marwah are of the symbols of Allah.'"
"236","Concerning His saying: 'and those who curse curse them' - 236 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: 'and those who curse curse them,' he said: The beasts, when the land becomes barren of rain, the beasts say: This is on account of the disobedient among the children of Adam; may Allah curse the disobedient among the children of Adam."
"237","237 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated, from Mansur, from Mujahid, concerning His Exalted saying: 'and those who curse curse them,' he said: The crawling creatures of the earth."
"238","238 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr, from 'Ata, concerning His Exalted saying: 'and those who curse curse them,' he said: The jinn and mankind and every crawling creature. [Flag: the chain names 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Bakr, which appears garbled; likely 'Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman.]"
"239","Concerning His saying: 'And your god is one God; there is no god but He, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate. Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth, and the alternation of night and day, and the ships that sail in the sea with what benefits people, and what Allah sent down from the sky of water and gave life thereby to the earth after its death, and dispersed therein of every creature, and the directing of winds and the clouds subjected between heaven and earth, are signs for a people who reason' - 239 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated: Sa'id ibn Masruq narrated, from Abu al-Duha, who said: When there was sent down: 'And your god is one God; there is no god but He, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate,' the associaters marveled and said: One god?! If he is truthful, let him bring us a sign. So Allah sent down: 'Indeed in the creation of the heavens and the earth and the alternation of night and day,' to His saying: 'for a people who reason.'"
"240","Concerning His saying: 'and the means were cut off from them' - 240 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Fudayl ibn 'Iyad narrated, from 'Ubayd al-Mukattib, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: 'and the means were cut off from them,' he said: The ties of kinship that were between them in this world."
"241","241 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Jarir ibn 'Abd al-Hamid narrated, from 'Ubayd al-Mukattib, from Mujahid, who said: The bond that was between them in this world."
"242","Concerning His saying: 'and do not follow the footsteps of Satan' - 242 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz, concerning His Almighty saying: 'and do not follow the footsteps of Satan,' he said: Vows in disobedience."
"243","Concerning His saying: 'But whoever is compelled, neither desiring nor transgressing, there is no sin upon him' - 243 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: 'But whoever is compelled, neither desiring nor transgressing,' he said: Neither desiring against the Muslims nor transgressing against them; whoever goes out severing kinship, or cutting the road, or corrupting in the earth, then is compelled to carrion, it is not lawful for him."
"244","Concerning His saying: 'How patient they are over the Fire' - 244 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: 'How patient they are over the Fire,' he said: How much they act with the deeds of the people of the Fire."
"245","Concerning His saying: 'and gives wealth, despite love for it, to relatives and orphans and the needy and the traveler and beggars and for freeing slaves' - 245 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Mus'ab ibn Mahan narrated, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Zubayd al-Iyami, from Murrah, from 'Abdullah, concerning His Almighty saying: 'and gives wealth, despite love for it,' he said: You give it while you are healthy and miserly, hoping for life and fearing poverty."
"246","Concerning His saying: 'O you who believed, prescribed upon you is retribution for the slain: the free for the free, the slave for the slave, the female for the female; but whoever is pardoned something from his brother, then following with kindness and payment to him with excellence; that is a lightening from your Lord and a mercy; but whoever transgresses after that, for him is a painful punishment' - 246 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, who said: Mujahid informed me, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Prescribed upon the Children of Israel was retribution for the slain, and there was no pardon among them. So Allah said to this nation: 'Prescribed upon you is retribution for the slain: the free for the free, the slave for the slave, the female for the female; but whoever is pardoned something from his brother, then following with kindness' - pardon is accepting blood-money for deliberate killing - 'that is a lightening from your Lord' - he said: A lightening of what was prescribed upon those before you - 'then following with kindness and payment to him with excellence.' And he said: This one follows with kindness, and this one pays to him with excellence."
"247","Concerning His saying: 'Prescribed upon you, when death presents itself to one of you, if he leaves good, is bequest for parents and near relatives with kindness, a duty upon the God-conscious' - 247 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Yunus narrated, from al-Hasan, concerning His Almighty saying: 'When death presents itself to one of you, if he leaves good, is bequest for parents and near relatives,' he said: Bequest was for parents and near relatives; then abrogated from that for parents, and their share was affirmed for them in Surat al-Nisa; and abrogated from near relatives every inheritor; and bequest remained for the near relatives who do not inherit."
"248","248 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from Muhammad ibn Sharik al-Makki, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from 'A'ishah, who said: A man said to her: I want to make a bequest. She said: How much is your wealth? He said: Three thousand. She said: How many are your dependents? He said: Four. She said: Allah Almighty said: 'if he leaves good.' And this thing is little; so leave it for your dependents, for that is better."
"249","249 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Isa ibn Yunus narrated, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, who said: There is no wealth greater in reward than wealth a man leaves for his children, enriching them from people."
"250","250 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated, he said: Ibn Jurayj narrated, from Layth, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: If the deceased leaves seven hundred dirhams, he does not make a bequest."
"251","251 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, he said: Hisham ibn 'Urwah narrated, from his father, who said: 'Ali visited a friend of his who was ill, and the man said to him: I want to make a bequest. 'Ali said to him: Allah the Exalted says: 'if he leaves good.' And you leave only a little thing; so leave it for your dependents, for that is better."
"252","252 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Yunus narrated, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he recited this verse upon the pulpit of Basra, then said: This has been abrogated."
"253","253 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, that he used to say: Bequest was before inheritance; then when inheritance was sent down, inheritance abrogated whoever inherits; and bequest remained for whoever does not inherit, so it is established. Whoever bequeaths to other than a relative, his bequest is not valid; for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No bequest is valid for an heir."
"254","254 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Yunus and Humayd narrated, from al-Hasan, that he used to say: Whoever bequeaths to other than his relatives, then for those he bequeathed to is a third of the third, and for his relatives two thirds of the third."
"255","Concerning His saying: 'But whoever fears from a testator deviation or sin and reconciles between them, there is no sin upon him; indeed Allah is Forgiving, Merciful' - 255 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Humayd, from Mujahid, that he used to recite: 'But whoever fears from a testator (musin) deviation.'"
"256","256 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Juwaybir narrated, from al-Dahhak, concerning His Almighty saying: 'But whoever fears from a testator deviation,' he said: Partiality, or deviation (janaf), is error, and sin is deliberate."
"257","257 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: That he bequeath to the child of his daughter while intending his daughter."
"258","258 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Deviation in bequest and harming therein are of the major sins."
"259","259 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid narrated, from Dawud, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Deviation in bequest and harming therein are of the major sins."
"260","260 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from 'Ikrimah, from Ibn 'Abbas, who said: Deviation or partiality in bequest, and harming therein, are of the major sins."
"261","Concerning His saying: 'O you who believed, prescribed upon you is fasting as it was prescribed upon those before you' - 261 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Sawwar ibn Abi Hakim, from 'Ata, concerning His Almighty saying: 'Prescribed upon you is fasting as it was prescribed upon those before you,' he said: Three days of every month."
"262","262 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from Hajjaj ibn Artah, from Abu Ja'far, who said: The month of Ramadan abrogated every fast."
"263","Concerning His saying: 'And upon those who can bear it is a ransom of feeding a poor person; so whoever volunteers good, it is better for him; and that you fast is better for you, if you know' - 263 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Ya'qub ibn 'Abd al-Rahman narrated, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Harmalah, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyab, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And upon those who can bear it is a ransom of feeding a poor person,' he said: It is the old man who used to fast it then became unable, and the pregnant woman for whom it is hard; upon both of them is feeding a poor person every day until the month of Ramadan ends."
"264","264 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Attab ibn Bashir narrated, from Khusayf, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And upon those who can bear it,' meaning: of those who reached (the age of) deeds so fasting became obligatory upon them; so whoever of these has an ailment of sickness, or sneezing, or has an ailment - man or excused woman - and abandons fasting - or the very old shaykh - upon him is a ransom of feeding a poor person for every day. 'So whoever volunteers good' - meaning he feeds every day two poor persons; 'and that you fast is better for you' - than that. [Flag: 'or sneezing' renders an odd word in the source ('utas); likely garbled for thirst or another ailment.]"
"265","265 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from 'Imran ibn Hudayr, from 'Ikrimah, that he used to recite: '(And upon those who are made to bear it),' saying: Had it been 'can bear it,' they would have fasted."
"266","266 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated, he said: 'Imran ibn Hudayr narrated, from 'Ikrimah, he used to recite: '(And upon those who are made to bear it),' and he recited: Those who can bear it are those who fast it, and those who are made to bear it are those who are weak; upon them is the ransom."
"267","267 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, from Ayyub and Khalid, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he recited Surat al-Baqarah upon the pulpit, explaining it; when he came to this verse he recited: 'feeding a poor person.'"
"268","268 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman narrated, from Shu'bah, from 'Amr ibn Murrah, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, who said: It is abrogated."
"269","269 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Layth narrated, from Tawus, from Ibn 'Abbas, that he used to recite: 'a ransom of feeding poor persons.'"
"270","270 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from 'Ubaydullah, from Nafi', from Ibn 'Umar, that he used to recite: 'a ransom of feeding poor persons.'"
"271","271 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from 'Abbad ibn Rashid and others, from al-Hasan, that he used to recite it likewise."
"272","Concerning His saying: 'and to complete the number' - 272 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: 'Abbad ibn Rashid informed me, he said: I heard al-Hasan recite: 'and to complete the number.'"
"273","Concerning His saying: 'So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it' - 273 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Shihab narrated, from Layth, from a man, from Ibn 'Umar, concerning this verse: 'So whoever of you witnesses the month, let him fast it,' he said: Whoever Ramadan reaches while among his family, then wants to travel, let him fast."
"274","274 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated, from his father, from Abu Mijlaz, who said: When the month of Ramadan arrives, let no one travel in it; but if he must do it, let him fast when he travels."
"275","Concerning His saying: 'Permitted to you on the night of fasting is intercourse with your wives' - 275 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from 'Amr ibn Dinar, from 'Ikrimah, who said: A man would eat and drink as long as he had not slept; then a man of the Muslims slept, so food and drink became forbidden to him until the like of it (the next night). Then a man transgressed two or three times, then the concession was sent down: 'Permitted to you on the night of fasting is intercourse with your wives.'"
"276","Concerning His saying: 'and seek what Allah has written for you' - 276 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated, from Shu'bah, who said: I asked al-Hakam about His Almighty saying: 'and seek what Allah has written for you,' he said: Meaning offspring."
"277","Concerning His saying: 'And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread of dawn; then complete fasting to the night' - 277 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Husayn narrated, from al-Sha'bi, from 'Adi ibn Hatim, who said: When there was sent down: 'And eat and drink until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread,' I took to two cords, white and black, and placed them under my pillow; I would stand in the night and could not distinguish the white from the black. In the morning I went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him; so he laughed and said: Your pillow is indeed wide; that is only the blackness of night and the whiteness of day."
"278","278 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His Almighty saying: 'until the white thread becomes distinct to you from the black thread,' he said: If a man takes suhur while seeing that night is upon him, though dawn had risen, let him complete his fast; for Allah says: 'And eat and drink until it becomes distinct to you.' And if he eats while seeing that the sun has set, though it had not set, let him make it up; for Allah the Exalted says: 'then complete fasting to the night.'"
"279","279 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Khalid and Mansur narrated, from Ibn Sirin, from Yahya ibn al-Jazzar, who said: Ibn Mas'ud was asked about a man who took suhur while seeing that night was upon him, though dawn had risen. He said: Whoever eats at the beginning of the day, let him eat at its end."
"280","280 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Mansur narrated, from Ibn Sirin, that he said the like of that; and al-Hasan said: He completes his fast and nothing is upon him."
"281","281 - Sa'id narrated, he said: 'Umar ibn 'Abd al-Wahid al-Sulami of the people of Damascus narrated, from al-Nu'man ibn al-Mundhir al-Ghassani, from Makhul, who said: Abu Sa'id al-Khudri was asked about a man who took suhur while seeing that night was upon him, though dawn had risen upon him. He said: If it is of the month of Ramadan, he fasts it and makes up a day in its place; and if it is of other than the month of Ramadan, let him eat at its end, for he ate at its beginning."
"282","Concerning His saying: 'And do not consume your wealth among you in falsehood, and present it to the rulers to consume a portion of people's wealth in sin while you know' - 282 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And do not consume your wealth among you in falsehood, and present it to the rulers,' he said: Do not dispute while you know you are a wrongdoer."
"283","Concerning His saying: 'And righteousness is not that you come to houses from their backs' - 283 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Mughirah narrated, from Ibrahim, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And righteousness is not that you come to houses from their backs,' he said: A man of the people of the Jahiliyyah, when he came to a house of the houses of some of his companions or his cousins, would lift the house from behind it - meaning houses of hair (tents) - then enter; so they were forbidden that and commanded to come to houses from their doors, then greet."
"284","Concerning His saying: 'And fight them until there is no fitnah and religion is for Allah' - 284 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn 'Abdullah narrated, from Bayan, from Wabarah ibn 'Abd al-Rahman, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, who said: 'Abdullah ibn 'Umar came out to us, and we hoped he would narrate to us a fine hadith; then a man hastened to him saying: O Abu 'Abd al-Rahman, what do you say about fighting in fitnah, while Allah says: 'And fight them until there is no fitnah'? Ibn 'Umar said: Do you know what fitnah is, may your mother be bereft of you? Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) only fought the associaters, and entering their religion was fitnah; it is not your fighting over kingship."
"285","Concerning His saying: 'And spend in the way of Allah, and do not throw yourselves with your hands into destruction' - 285 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Hudhayfah, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And do not throw yourselves with your hands into destruction,' he said: Abandoning spending."
"286","286 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Ibn Abi Najih or another, from Mujahid, who said: By your withholding spending in the way of Allah, fearing poverty."
"287","Concerning His saying: 'And complete Hajj and 'Umrah for Allah; but if you are prevented, then whatever is feasible of sacrificial animals; and do not shave your heads until the sacrificial animal reaches its place; so whoever of you is ill or has an ailment of his head, then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice; then when you are secure, whoever enjoys 'Umrah until Hajj, then whatever is feasible of sacrificial animals; but whoever does not find, then fasting three days in Hajj and seven when you return' - 287 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated, he said: al-A'mash narrated, from Ibrahim, from 'Alqamah, concerning His Almighty saying: 'And complete Hajj and 'Umrah for Allah,' he said: It is in the recitation of 'Abdullah: '(to the House).' He said: Do not pass beyond the House with 'Umrah. Then if you are prevented: when a man enters ihram for Hajj then is prevented, he sends whatever is feasible of sacrificial animals. If he hastens before the sacrificial animal reaches its place and shaves his head, or touches perfume, or treats himself with medicine, upon him is a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice; fasting is three days, charity is three sa' for six poor persons, half a sa' for each poor person, and sacrifice is a sheep. 'Then when you are secure' - meaning: when he recovers, then proceeds from that journey to the House, he exits his Hajj state with an 'Umrah, and upon him is Hajj the next year. If he returns without completing from that journey to the House, upon him are a Hajj and an 'Umrah and blood for delaying the 'Umrah. If he returns performing tamattu' in the months of Hajj, upon him is whatever is feasible of sacrificial animals - a sheep; and if he does not find, then fasting three days in Hajj and seven when you return. Ibrahim said: He makes the last of the fasting of three days in Hajj the Day of 'Arafah. Ibrahim said: I mentioned this hadith to Sa'id ibn Jubayr, and he said: Thus did Ibn 'Abbas say in all of this hadith."
"288","288 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Yahya ibn Zakariyya narrated, from Ibn 'Awn, from al-Sha'bi, that he used to recite: 'And complete (wa-atimmu) Hajj and 'Umrah for Allah.'"
"289","289 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu 'Awanah narrated, from 'Abd al-Rahman al-Asbahani, from 'Abdullah ibn Ma'qil, who said: We were sitting in the mosque when Ka'b ibn 'Ujrah sat with us, saying: Concerning me this verse was sent down: 'So whoever of you is ill or has an ailment of his head.' He said: I said: How was your affair? He said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in ihram, when lice fell upon my head, my beard and my mustache, until they fell upon my eyebrows. So I mentioned that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: I did not think hardship had reached you to this extent; call the barber. So the barber came and shaved my head. He said: Do you find (means for) a sacrifice? I said: No - and it is a sheep. He said: Then fast three days, or feed three sa' among six poor persons. He said: And it was sent down concerning me specifically, and it is for people generally."
"290","290 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Abu Bishr narrated, from Mujahid, from 'Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn 'Ujrah, who said: This verse was sent down concerning me. He said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Hudaybiyah, in ihram, and the associaters had confined us; and I had thick hair, so vermin began falling upon my face. So there was sent down: 'then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice.' The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do the vermin of your head harm you? I said: Yes. He said: Then shave, and fast three days, or feed six poor persons, or sacrifice a sacrificed animal."
"291","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, Ibn Abi Najih and Ayyub narrated to us, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Ka'b ibn Ujrah: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was kindling a fire under a cooking pot of his and lice were falling onto his face, and he said: Do your vermin trouble you? He said: I said: Yes. He said: Shave your head and offer a sacrifice, or fast three days, or feed a faraq (of food) among six poor persons. Sa'id narrated to us, he said: And Sufyan said: Ibn Abi Najih said: slaughter a sheep, and Ayyub said: offer a sacrifice."
"292","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Mujahid, he said: Ka'b ibn Ujrah said: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, this verse was revealed concerning me, and it was me that was meant by it: (But whoever among you is ill or has an ailment of the head, then a ransom of fasting or charity or sacrifice). We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at al-Hudaybiyah while we were in ihram, and the enemy had besieged us, and I had a full head of hair, and vermin were falling onto my face. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by me and said: It is as though the vermin of your head trouble you? I said: Yes. He said: Then shave, and the verse was revealed."
"293","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Qilabah, he said: Ka'b ibn Ujrah said: I became lousy until I thought that between the tip of every hair of my head there was a louse and nits, and I had fine hair. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Give in charity three sa's (of food) to six poor persons."
"294","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim and Mujahid, they both said: The fasting is three days, the charity is upon six poor persons, and the sacrifice is a sheep or more."
"295","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: The fasting is ten days, the charity is upon ten poor persons, and the sacrifice is a sheep or more."
"296","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from Nafi', he said: Sulayman ibn Yasar narrated to us that Umar asked Ibn Ka'b ibn Ujrah: What did your father do about the trouble that afflicted him? He said: He slaughtered a cow."
"297","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Qurashi narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Abi Sa'id, from Abu Hurayrah: that Ka'b ibn Ujrah slaughtered a sheep for the trouble that had afflicted him."
"298","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Abbas, he said concerning (whatever sacrificial animal is easily available): (It is) a sheep, even up to (the worth of) shackles. [Flag: the phrase hatta al-quyud is unclear in the source]"
"299","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yahya ibn Sa'id narrated to us, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Aishah and Ibn Umar: concerning (whatever sacrificial animal is easily available), they both said: A she-camel of lesser quality than a (prime) she-camel, and a cow of lesser quality than a (prime) cow."
"300","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A camel, or even two camels, may be easily affordable for a man."
"301","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Ja'far ibn Muhammad, from his father, from Ali concerning (whatever sacrificial animal is easily available), he said: A sheep."
"302","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A sheep."
"303","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr concerning (whatever sacrificial animal is easily available), he said: A sheep."
"304","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ata. [Flag: the matn is missing and the report is truncated in the source]"
"305","And Yunus and Mansur (narrated to us), from al-Hasan. [Flag: truncated chain fragment in the source; the matn follows in no. 308]"
"306","And Mughirah (narrated to us), from Ibrahim. [Flag: truncated chain fragment in the source; the matn follows in no. 308]"
"307","And Juwaybir (narrated to us), from al-Dahhak. [Flag: truncated chain fragment in the source; the matn follows in no. 308]"
"308","And Abu Bishr (narrated to us), from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: that they said: Whatever is easily available of the sacrificial animals is a sheep."
"309","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Our companions used to say: Whatever is easily available of the sacrificial animals is a sheep."
"310","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Amr ibn al-Harith, from Ubaydullah ibn Abi Usayd, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, that he said: Are you inclined toward these two shaykhs, Ibn Abbas and Ibn al-Zubayr, who differ in fatwa? Ibn al-Zubayr said: A sheep is only an ordinary slaughter (not a sacrificial animal), and Ibn Abbas said: It is whatever is easily available of the sacrificial animals. I said: Which of the two is more correct? He said: I heard Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: It is whatever is easily available of the sacrificial animals."
"311","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Abi Ishaq narrated to us, he said: I heard Mujahid narrate from Ibn Abbas, he said: From the eight pairs (of livestock)."
"312","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from Ata ibn Abi Rabah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: From the eight pairs: of camels, cattle, sheep and goats, according to what is easily affordable; whatever is greater is more excellent."
"313","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Awn informed me, from Marwan ibn al-Asfar, from Ibn Umar, he said: It was said to him: Does a sheep suffice for the one performing tamattu'? Ibn Umar said: All of you (say) a sheep? - twice - Would any one of you be pleased that he has nothing with Allah on the Day of Resurrection but a sheep?"
"314","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Ghaylan ibn Jarir, he said: I was with Ibn Umar when a man asked him: Does a sheep suffice for the one performing tamattu'? He said: All of you (say) a sheep? - as though repeating it - and he disliked it for tamattu'."
"315","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from Wabarah, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: Fasting is dearer to me for the one performing tamattu' than a sheep."
"316","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Attab ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar concerning (whatever sacrificial animal is easily available), he said: A cow. And Ibn Abbas said: A sheep."
"317","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Zuhri narrated to us; he was asked about whatever is easily available of the sacrificial animals, and he said: Ibn Umar said: Of camels and cattle, and Ibn Abbas said: Of sheep and goats."
"318","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas say: Whatever is easily available of the sacrificial animals is a sheep, or a she-camel, or a cow, or a share in (sacrificial) blood."
"319","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Abbad al-Muhallabi narrated to us, he said: Abu Jamrah narrated to us, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about tamattu' in Hajj, and he enjoined it upon me; and I asked him about the slaughter, and he said: A she-camel, or a cow, or a sheep, or a share in (sacrificial) blood."
"320","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ata, he said: The camel and the cow are on behalf of seven; those offering the udhiyah sacrifice, those performing tamattu', and those prevented (from completing Hajj) share in it."
"321","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah and Hushaym narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: Whoever does not fast the three days that are during the Hajj, the last of them being the Day of Arafah, then the sacrificial animal has become obligatory upon him. Abu Bishr said: So I said to Sa'id: And if he cannot find (one)? He said: Then let him sell his garment. And Hushaym added: And buy a sheep for three dirhams."
"322","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid and from Tawus, they both said concerning the one performing tamattu', he said: If he wishes he may fast a day of Shawwal, a day of Dhu al-Qa'dah, and a day of Dhu al-Hijjah."
"323","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ata, he said: He fasts only during the ten (days of Dhu al-Hijjah); and if the fasting escapes him, he sheds blood (offers a sacrifice)."
"324","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, he said: The one performing tamattu' fasts during the journey, and he fasts only during the ten, making the last of them the Day of Arafah; and if it escapes him, he sheds blood for that."
"325","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us: Abd al-Malik and Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ata, concerning His saying (and seven when you return), he said: It is a concession, and if he wishes he may fast during the journey."
"326","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, the like of the saying of Ata, he said: It is a concession."
"327","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Jabir, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When you return to your town."
"328","His Exalted saying: (Hajj is during well-known months). 328 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (Hajj is during well-known months), he said: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and ten nights of Dhu al-Hijjah. From Ibn Umar, he said: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and Dhu al-Hijjah."
"329","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, he said: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and Dhu al-Hijjah."
"330","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, he said: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and Dhu al-Hijjah."
"331","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and ten (days) of Dhu al-Hijjah."
"332","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah and Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim and al-Sha'bi, that they both said: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and ten (days) of Dhu al-Hijjah."
"333","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, the like of that."
"334","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn Wahb narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn al-Harith informed me, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Nawfal, that he heard Urwah ibn al-Zubayr say: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said concerning (Hajj is during well-known months), he said: Shawwal, Dhu al-Qa'dah, and Dhu al-Hijjah."
"335","His Exalted saying: (So whoever undertakes Hajj therein, then there is to be no sexual relations, nor disobedience, nor disputing during Hajj). 335 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, he said: al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab narrated to us, from Ata, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (So whoever undertakes Hajj therein), he said: The undertaking of Hajj is the talbiyah."
"336","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His saying: (and no disputing during Hajj), he said: There is no disputing in Hajj, nor doubt, nor forgetfulness (?) [Flag: wa-la nisyan is unclear in source] in Hajj; Hajj is in Dhu al-Hijjah."
"337","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ata, he said: The undertaking of Hajj is the talbiyah."
"338","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (then no sexual relations), he said: The rafath mentioned here is not the rafath that you mentioned: (Sexual relations with you are permitted on the night of fasting); it is al-arabah - in the speech of the Arabs - and alluding to mention of marriage."
"339","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that he said: Rafath is intercourse, fusuq is acts of disobedience, and jidal is quarrelling."
"340","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik narrated to us, from Ata, he said: I heard him say: Rafath is intercourse, fusuq is acts of disobedience, and jidal is quarrelling in Hajj until they become angry."
"341","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us: Hajjaj informed us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that he said that."
"342","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan. [Flag: matn omitted in source; the saying follows in no. 343]"
"343","And Mughirah (narrated to us), from Ibrahim; they both said: Rafath is intercourse, fusuq is acts of disobedience, and jidal is quarrelling."
"344","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, he said: Rafath is intercourse, fusuq is acts of disobedience to Allah, Mighty and Majestic, and jidal is contention and quarrelling."
"345","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Husayn, from his father, he said: Ibn Abbas dismounted from his riding animal and began to lead it, chanting rajaz and saying: hamisan (?) [Flag: a word in the verse is unclear in the source] mentioning intercourse without euphemism. I said: O Abu Abbas, do you speak rafath while you are in ihram?! He said: Rafath is whatever women are addressed with (of such talk)."
"346","His Exalted saying: (And take provisions, but indeed the best provision is piety. And fear Me, O you of understanding). 346 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, he said: There were people who would perform Hajj without taking provisions, saying: We rely upon Allah, and He is our Provider. So there was revealed: (And take provisions, but indeed the best provision is piety)."
"347","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, he said: There were people who would perform Hajj without provisions, so there was revealed: (And take provisions, but indeed the best provision is piety)."
"348","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Ata, from al-Sha'bi, he said: (It means) cakes and sawiq (parched barley meal)."
"349","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Suqah, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (And take provisions), he said: Cakes and oil."
"350","His Exalted saying: (There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord). 350 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, he said: Ibn Abbas said: Ukaz, Dhu al-Majaz and al-Majannah were markets in the pre-Islamic period; then when Islam came they felt sinful about selling in them, so there was revealed: (There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord) - during the seasons of Hajj."
"351","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, he said: They would not trade during the days of Mina and the Day of Arafah, so Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: (There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord) to the end of the verse."
"352","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from al-Ala ibn al-Musayyab, he said: A man informed me that he asked Ibn Umar, saying: O Abdullah, we are people who hire out (camels) in this direction, and some people claim we have no Hajj? He said to him: A man asked the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about what you asked about, and he did not answer him anything until this verse was revealed: (There is no blame upon you for seeking bounty from your Lord). So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the man and said: You are pilgrims."
"353","His Exalted saying: (Then remember Allah at al-Mash'ar al-Haram). 353 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (Then remember Allah at al-Mash'ar al-Haram), he said: The small mountain (of Muzdalifah) and what is around it."
"354","His Exalted saying: (And remember Allah during numbered days). 354 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: The known days are the ten days (of Dhu al-Hijjah), and the numbered days are the days of tashriq."
"355","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The numbered days are the days of tashriq."
"356","His Exalted saying: (But whoever hastens in two days, there is no sin upon him; and whoever delays, there is no sin upon him, for whoever fears Allah). 356 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (But whoever hastens in two days, there is no sin upon him): in his hastening on the second day, and no sin upon him in delaying to the third day."
"357","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: There is no sin upon him in hastening, and no sin upon him in delaying."
"358","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Maryam, he said: I heard Mujahid say concerning (But whoever hastens in two days, there is no sin upon him; and whoever delays, there is no sin upon him), he said: All of them are forgiven."
"359","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Abbad ibn Rashid, he said: I heard al-Hasan say: Allah knew that it (Mina) is a land of عرض (?) [Flag: balad ard is unclear in the source], so He gave concession to His servants: whoever wishes may depart in the first departure, and whoever wishes in the latter departure."
"360","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Ilaqah, from al-Ma'rur ibn Suwayd, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever wishes to depart in the first departure, let him depart, except the Banu Khuzaymah."
"361","His Exalted saying: (And of the people is he whose speech pleases you in worldly life, and he calls Allah to witness as to what is in his heart, yet he is the fiercest of opponents). 361 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, he said: A man came to him and said: We find in some of the books: that Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has servants whose tongues are sweeter than honey and whose hearts are more bitter than aloes; they wear for people sheep's clothing of softness, and they seek the world through religion. Allah says: Do they dare against Me? Are they deluded by Me? By My Might, I shall surely let loose upon them a tribulation that leaves the forbearing one bewildered. Muhammad ibn Ka'b said: This is in the Book of Allah: (And of the people is he whose speech pleases you in worldly life, and he calls Allah to witness as to what is in his heart, yet he is the fiercest of opponents). The man said: We know concerning whom it was revealed. Muhammad said to him: A matter is revealed concerning a man, then it becomes general."
"362","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Abu Ubaydah, he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, says: What is with people who acquire understanding for other than My worship, wearing sheep's clothing, their hearts more bitter than aloes? Are they deluded by Me? Or do they try to deceive Me? By Myself I have sworn: I shall surely let loose upon them a tribulation that leaves the forbearing one bewildered therein."
"363","His Exalted saying: (Fighting has been prescribed for you while it is hateful to you). 363 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (Fighting has been prescribed for you while it is hateful to you), he said: This verse abrogated it: (They said: We hear and we obey). [Flag: the abrogating verse is given in truncated form in the source]"
"364","His Exalted saying: (And they ask you what they should spend. Say: The surplus). 364 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (And they ask you what they should spend. Say: The surplus), he said: The excess."
"365","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The excess beyond (the needs of) one's dependents."
"366","His Exalted saying: (Your wives are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage however you wish). 366 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, he said: The Jews said: A child is only born cross-eyed if a man comes to his wife from behind her. So Allah, Mighty and Majestic, sent down: (Your wives are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage however you wish): from before her and from behind her, but let him not come to her except in the place of coming (the vagina)."
"367","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: I heard Jabir say: The Jews used to say: Whoever comes to his wife from her behind into her front, the child will be cross-eyed. So there was revealed: (Your wives are a tillage for you, so come to your tillage however you wish)."
"368","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Usamah ibn al-Had, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Husayn, from Harami ibn Abdullah al-Waqifi, from Khuzaymah ibn Thabit: that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah is not shy of the truth: do not come to women in their backsides."
"369","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: Yazid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Umarah ibn Khuzaymah, from his father, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Indeed Allah is not shy of the truth: do not come to women in their backsides."
"370","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Abu Abdullah al-Shaqari narrated to me, he said: Abu al-Qa'qa' narrated to me, he said: I was present at al-Qadisiyyah while a boy - or a youth - he said: A man came to Abdullah and said: May I come to my wife however I wish? He said: Yes. He said: And wherever I wish? He said: Yes. He said: And however I wish? He said: Yes. He said: Then a (certain) man understood what he meant and said: He means to come to her in her seat (anus). He said: No; the posteriors of women are forbidden to you."
"371","His Exalted saying: (And do not make Allah an excuse in your oaths to keep from doing good and acting piously and making peace among people. And Allah is Hearing, Knowing). 371 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (And do not make Allah an excuse in your oaths), he said: It is the man who swears that he will not join his kin, nor do good to his relatives, nor make peace between two persons; so his oath does not prevent him from doing that, and he expiates for his oath."
"372","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, the like of that."
"373","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: He joins his kin, does good to his relatives, and makes peace among people, and there is no expiation upon him; and had I enjoined expiation upon him I would have enjoined him not to fulfil his word. [Flag: the closing clause is obscure in the source]"
"374","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, concerning this verse, he said: It is the man who swears that he will not join kin, nor fear Allah, nor make peace between two persons."
"375","His Exalted saying: (For those who swear not to touch their wives is a waiting of four months; but if they return, then indeed Allah is Forgiving, Merciful. And if they resolve on divorce, then indeed Allah is Hearing, Knowing). 375 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, he said: Ibn Abbas used to recite: (For those who swear an oath away from their wives is a waiting of four months); (and if they resolve on release)."
"376","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Layla narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The resolve of divorce is the expiry of the four months, and al-fay' (return) is intercourse."
"377","His Exalted saying: (And there is no blame upon you for what you hint of proposal to women, or conceal within yourselves. Allah knows that you will mention them, but do not promise them secretly, except that you say a proper word). 377 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: I heard him say concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (but do not promise them secretly), he said: Do not take her covenant that she will not marry other than you, and do not confirm the عقد (marriage contract) until the waiting period expires."
"378","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from al-Hasan. [Flag: matn omitted in source; the saying follows in no. 381]"
"379","And Juwaybir (narrated to us), from al-Dahhak. [Flag: matn omitted in source; the saying follows in no. 381]"
"380","And al-Taymi (narrated to us), from Abu Mijlaz. [Flag: matn omitted in source; the saying follows in no. 381]"
"381","And one informing (narrated to us), from Ibrahim ibn Muhajir, and he narrates from Ata: that they said: It is fornication."
"382","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, concerning His saying, Exalted: (but do not promise them secretly), he said: He is not to propose to her during her waiting period. (Except that you say a proper word): he says: You are indeed beautiful, and you are indeed of (noble) standing, and you are indeed desired."
"383","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Mansur, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (And there is no blame upon you for what you hint of proposal to women), he said: Hinting (ta'rid) is whatever does not set out plainly for proposal."
"384","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, he said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, from Muslim al-Batin, from Ibn Jubayr, he said: He says: I intend to marry, and if I marry I shall treat my wife well."
"385","His Exalted saying: (And if you divorce them before you have touched them, while you have already appointed for them an appointed dower, then half of what you appointed, except that they pardon, or he pardons in whose hand is the marriage tie. And that you pardon is nearer to piety). 385 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, he said: Shurayh used to say: The one in whose hand is the marriage tie is the husband."
"386","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, he said: It is the guardian."
"387","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, he said: It is the guardian."
"388","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Tawus and Ata and the people of Madinah: that they said: The one in whose hand is the marriage tie is the guardian. So I informed them of the saying of Sa'id ibn Jubayr: it is the husband, and they retracted their saying. Then when Sa'id ibn Jubayr came he said: Do you see: if the guardian pardons but the woman refuses, what does the guardian's pardon amount to? Or if she pardons but the guardian refuses, what has the guardian to do with that?"
"389","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, he said: Allah, Mighty and Majestic, enjoined pardoning and permitted it; so if she pardons, her pardon is valid, and if she withholds and her guardian pardons, his pardon is valid."
"390","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, he said: A man of ours married a woman, then her husband divorced her before consummating with her, and her brother pardoned (waived) her dower. They raised the matter to Shurayh and he allowed his pardoning. Then he said afterwards: I pardon (waive) the dower of the Banu Murrah. And he used to say afterwards: The one in whose hand is the marriage tie is the husband: that he pardons the whole dower and hands it over to her, or she pardons half of what Allah, Mighty and Majestic, appointed for her; and if they both withhold, she has half the dower."
"391","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, he said: By Allah, Shurayh never gave a judgment more foolish than this one, when he abandoned his first saying and adopted this one."
"392","His Exalted saying: (Maintain the prayers, and the middle prayer, and stand before Allah devoutly obedient). 392 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Zirr ibn Hubaysh, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the Day of the Trench: May Allah fill their graves and their hearts with fire, as they kept us busy from the middle prayer, and it is the Asr prayer."
"393","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Muslim ibn Subayh, from Shutayr ibn Shakal, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the Day of the Confederates: They kept us busy from the middle prayer, the Asr prayer; may Allah fill their houses and their graves with fire. Then he prayed it between Maghrib and Isha."
"394","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Abu Hayyan al-Taymi, from his father, he said: A man asked Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, about the middle prayer, and he did not answer him anything; then the Asr prayer was established, and when he finished he said: Where is the questioner about the middle prayer? He said: It is me, here. He said: It is this prayer."
"395","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Salih, he said: Abu Hurayrah said: The middle prayer is the Asr prayer."
"396","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Uthman ibn Khuthaym, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Labibah al-Ta'ifi, he said: I said to Abu Hurayrah: The middle prayer? He said: Why, it is the Asr prayer."
"397","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: The middle prayer is the Fajr prayer."
"398","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from Zayd ibn Aslam, he said: I heard Ibn Umar say: It is the Fajr prayer."
"399","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, he said: It is the Fajr prayer."
"400","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, he said: It is the Asr prayer."
"401","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Attab ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam: that Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, ordered a mushaf of hers to be written, and said: When you reach (Maintain the prayers), do not write it until you inform me. When they informed her that they had reached it, she said: Write it: the middle prayer is the Asr prayer."
"402","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Attab narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: It is the Fajr prayer."
"403","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, I think from Abu Ishaq, he said: One who heard Ibn Abbas narrated to me, saying concerning (Maintain the prayers, and the middle prayer), he said: It is the Asr prayer."
"404","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Abayah ibn Rifa'ah, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (and stand before Allah devoutly obedient), that is: obedient."
"405","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us ... and lowering the hands, and lowering the gaze in prayer. [Flag: the beginning of the report is missing in the source]"
"406","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: Part of qunut (devout obedience) is bowing, humility, lowering the gaze, and lowering the wing out of awe of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. When one of the scholars stood in prayer, he would revere the Most Merciful, glorified and exalted is He, too much to extend his gaze, or fiddle with anything, or turn, or turn over the pebbles, or speak to himself about any affair of the world, except forgetfully."
"407","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah while people were talking in prayer about their needs, just as the People of the Book talk in prayer about their needs, until this verse was revealed: (and stand before Allah devoutly obedient)."
"408","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated to us, from al-Harith ibn Shubayl, from Abu Amr al-Shaybani, from Zayd ibn Arqam, he said: We used to talk in prayer; one of us would speak to the one beside him. Then there was revealed: (and stand before Allah devoutly obedient), and we were commanded to be silent and forbidden from speech."
"409","His Exalted saying: (And if you fear, then on foot or riding; but when you are secure, remember Allah as He taught you what you did not know). 409 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (And if you fear, then on foot or riding), he said: That is in fighting: that a man prays wherever his face happens to be, and upon his mount wherever it happens to face, gesturing with his head."
"410","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, he said: I asked Ibrahim about His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (then on foot or riding), he said: At the time of pursuit, he prays wherever his face happens to be, riding or on foot, two rak'ahs, gesturing; making the prostration lower than the bowing."
"411","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us: Yunus narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: He prays one rak'ah wherever his face happens to be, gesturing."
"412","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir narrated to us, from al-Dahhak, he said: When it is at the time of crossing swords, or he is pursuing or a beast of prey is pursuing him, let him pray one rak'ah (?) [Flag: rak'ah rak'ah is unclear in source] wherever his face happens to be, gesturing; and if he cannot, then let him utter one takbirah, or two takbirahs."
"413","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Shu'ayb ibn Dinar, he said: I heard Abd al-Wahhab ibn Bukht al-Makki say: When it is the crossing of swords, if they are able they pray standing; otherwise riding; otherwise the takbir; and if they cannot, let them not leave its remembrance in their hearts (?) [Flag: the closing clause is obscure in the source]."
"414","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from al-Awza'i, he said: Sabiq al-Barbari narrated to me, he said: Makhul wrote to al-Hasan, while we were with him at Dabiq, concerning people who are pursued. His letter came: If they are not being pursued, they pray on the ground; and if they are being pursued, they pray upon their mounts."
"415","His Exalted saying: (And those who are taken in death among you and leave wives: a bequest for their wives, provision for a year without expulsion). 415 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir narrated to us, from al-Dahhak, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (And those who are taken in death among you and leave wives: a bequest for their wives, provision for a year without expulsion), he said: When a man died, maintenance was spent upon his wife for a year, then the people of inheritance would divide their inheritance. Then there was revealed: (And those who are taken in death among you and leave wives shall wait concerning themselves four months and ten days). Then there was abrogated, from the four months and ten, (and those with pregnancies, their term is that they deliver their burden); when they deliver, within less than that."
"416","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Abbas, that he recited this verse: (And those who are taken in death among you and leave wives: a bequest for their wives, provision for a year), he said: This has been abrogated."
"417","His Exalted saying: (Who is it that will lend Allah a goodly loan so He will multiply it for him many times over?). 417 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: When there was revealed: (Who is it that will lend Allah a goodly loan), Abu al-Dahdah said: O Messenger of Allah, does Allah want a loan from us? He said: Yes, O Abu al-Dahdah. He said: Show me your hand. He handed him his hand. He said: I have lent my Lord my garden - and in his garden were six hundred date-palms. Then he came to the garden and said: O Umm al-Dahdah - while she was in the garden - and she said: At your service. He said: Come out, for I have lent it to my Lord, Mighty and Majestic."
"418","His Exalted saying: (And their prophet said to them: Indeed the sign of his kingship is that the Ark will come to you, in it tranquility from your Lord and a remnant of what the family of Musa and the family of Harun left, carried by the angels). 418 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar: that Uthman ibn Affan ordered the youths of the Muhajirun and the Ansar to write the masahif. He said: Whatever you differ in, put it in the tongue of Quraysh. The Muhajirun said: al-Tabut, and the Ansar said: al-Tabuh. Uthman said: Write it in the language of the Muhajirun: al-Tabut."
"419","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Jabir ibn Samurah, he said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: None shall take charge of our masahif except the youths of Quraysh and Thaqif."
"420","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Isa ibn Umar, from al-Suddi, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (tranquility from your Lord), he said: A basin of gold in which the hearts of the prophets are washed."
"421","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu Malik, from Ibn Abbas, he said: A basin of gold in which the hearts of the prophets are washed."
"422","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abdullah ibn al-Mubarak narrated to us, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid, from Abu Salih, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (and a remnant of what the family of Musa and the family of Harun left), he said: In it were the staff of Musa, the staff of Harun, the garments of Musa, the garments of Harun, two tablets of the Torah, and the manna."
"423","His Exalted saying: (except one who scoops a scoop with his hand). 423 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sadaqah ibn Khalid al-Dimashqi narrated to us, from Yahya ibn al-Harith al-Dhimari, he said: One who heard Uthman ibn Affan recite narrated to me: (except one who scoops a scoop)."
"424","His Exalted saying: (Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Self-Subsisting. Neither slumber nor sleep overtakes Him. To Him belongs whatever is in the heavens and whatever is on earth. Who is it that can intercede with Him except by His permission? He knows what is before them and what is behind them, and they encompass nothing of His knowledge except what He wills. His Kursi extends over the heavens and the earth, and guarding them does not tire Him. And He is the Most High, the Most Great). 424 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hakim ibn Jubayr, from Abu Salih, from Abu Hurayrah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything has a hump, and the hump of the Quran is Surah al-Baqarah; and in it is a verse, the chief of the verses of the Quran: it is not recited in a house in which there is a devil but he leaves it."
"425","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Mujahid, he said: The heavens and the earth in the Kursi are but like a ring cast in a desert land."
"426","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from al-Sha'bi, from Shutayr ibn Shakal, he said: Abdullah narrated to us: The greatest verse in the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is: (Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Self-Subsisting. Neither slumber nor sleep overtakes Him) to the end of the verse. Masruq said: You have spoken the truth."
"427","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, he said: Asim ibn Bahdalah narrated to us, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, he said: I heard Abdullah ibn Mas'ud say: There is no heaven, nor earth, nor plain, nor mountain greater than Ayat al-Kursi. Shutayr said: And I had heard it."
"428","His Exalted saying: (There is no compulsion in religion). 428 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (There is no compulsion in religion), he said: It was revealed concerning the Ansar. He said: I said: Specifically? He said: Specifically. A woman among them, when she was one who seldom bore or whose children did not survive, would vow: if she bears a child she will place him among the Jews, seeking thereby his long survival. Then Islam came while among them were some of them. When al-Nadir were expelled, the Ansar said: O Messenger of Allah, our sons and our brothers are among them? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was silent toward them, then there was revealed: (There is no compulsion in religion). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your companions have been given a choice: if they choose you, they are of you; and if they choose them, then expel (?) [Flag: fa-ajluhum is obscure in the source] them along with them."
"429","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: He had a slave boy called Jarir (?) [Flag: name uncertain in source], and he used to say to him: Accept Islam; thus it used to be said to them. And some people of the Ansar had been suckled among Qurayzah, and they used to say to them: Accept Islam, so there was revealed: (There is no compulsion in religion)."
"430","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Wa'il ibn Dawud, from al-Hasan, concerning His saying, Mighty and Majestic: (There is no compulsion in religion), he said: The People of the Book are not compelled to (accept) Islam."
"431","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Abu Hilal, from Wasaq (?) [Flag: name garbled in the source], he said: I was a slave of Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and I was a Christian. He used to say to me: O Wasaq (?), accept Islam, for if you accept Islam I shall put you in charge of some of the affairs of the Muslims, for it is not proper that one who is not of their religion should be in charge of their affair. I refused him, and he said to me: (There is no compulsion in religion). When Umar died, he freed me. [Flag: the freeing is attributed to Umar though he had died; the source may mean his will]"
"432","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Wuhayb, freedman of Zayd ibn Thabit, he said: Zayd ibn Thabit freed a Magian slave boy of his called Mabura, and I saw him with my father cutting roast meat."
"433","His Exalted saying: (The right path has become distinct from error). 433 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, that he used to recite: (The right path has become distinct from error), and he used to say: My recitation is upon the recitation of Mujahid."
"434","His Exalted saying: (Or like the one who passed by a town while it had fallen into ruin upon its roofs. He said: How will Allah give life to this after its death? So Allah caused him to die for a hundred years, then raised him) to His Exalted saying: (He said: I know that Allah is over all things competent). 434 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hazm ibn Abi Hazm narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Hasan say concerning this verse: (Or like the one who passed by a town while it had fallen into ruin upon its roofs. He said: How will Allah give life to this after its death? So Allah caused him to die for a hundred years, then raised him), he said: It was mentioned to us that he was made to die in the forenoon and raised when the sun declined before it set. He said: (How long did you remain? He said: I remained a day or part of a day. He said: Rather you remained a hundred years. So look at your food and your drink, they have not changed; and look at your donkey; and that We may make you a sign for people) - and your donkey, We shall surely give it life - and your food and your drink, Allah, Mighty and Majestic, has kept beasts of prey away from them; (and look at the bones, how We raise them then clothe them with flesh). It was mentioned to me that the first thing Allah, Mighty and Majestic, created of him (in raising him) was his two eyes, and he began to look with them at bone after bone, how it returns to its place. (So when it became clear to him, he said: I know that Allah is over all things competent)."
"435","Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, he said: Ibn Abbas used to recite: (Know that Allah is over all things competent), and say: He was not better than Ibrahim. Allah said: (Know that Allah is over all things competent)."
"436","436 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated to us, from his father, from Kharijah ibn Zayd ibn Thabit, that he used to recite: {Nunshizuha} (We raise them to life)."
"437","437 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from Abu al-Aliyah, from one of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he used to recite: {Nunshizuha}."
"438","438 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Umayr ibn Qu'aym, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite: {Nunshiruha} (We spread them / give them life)."
"439","439 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that he used to recite it likewise."
"440","440 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Hilal, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite: {Kayfa nunshiruha} (how We give them life)."
"441","His Almighty saying: {He said: Did you not believe? He said: Yes, but so that my heart may be at rest} 441 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Layth narrated to us, from Mujahid and Ibrahim, that they both said regarding the Almighty saying {so that my heart may be at rest} - they said: So that I increase in faith added to my faith."
"442","442 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Amr ibn Thabit al-Haddad narrated to us, from his father, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, regarding the Almighty saying {so that my heart may be at rest} - he said: By intimate friendship (al-khullah)."
"443","His Almighty saying: {Then take four birds and incline them toward you, then place on each mountain a portion of them, then call them, they will come to you hastening, and know that Allah is Mighty, Wise} 443 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Abu Jamrah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying regarding the Almighty saying {then take four birds and incline them toward you} - he said: Cut their wings into quarters, a quarter here and a quarter here in the quarters of the earth; {then call them, they will come to you hastening} he said: This is a parable; thus Allah gives life to the dead like this."
"444","444 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, regarding the Almighty saying {then incline them toward you} - he said: Cut them up."
"445","His Almighty saying: {O you who believe, spend from the good things you earned and from what We brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend} 445 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: One who heard al-Hakam narrating from Mujahid informed me, regarding the Almighty saying {spend from the good things you earned} - he said: From trade; {and from what We brought forth for you from the earth} he said: From fruits. [Flag: one link in the chain is unnamed in the source]"
"446","446 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from al-Hakam, from Mujahid, the like thereof."
"447","447 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: I asked Abidah about the Almighty saying {spend from the good things you earned and from what We brought forth for you from the earth, and do not aim at the bad thereof to spend} he said: That is concerning zakat, and a counterfeit dirham is dearer to me than a date."
"448","His Almighty saying: {And whoever is given wisdom has been given much good} 448 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Mujahid, regarding the Almighty saying {and whoever is given wisdom has been given much good} - he said: Wisdom is correctness."
"449","His Almighty saying: {And He will expiate from you your evil deeds} 449 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Hanzalah al-Sadusi, from Ikrimah, that he used to recite: {And We will expiate from you your evil deeds}."
"450","His Almighty saying: {Those who devour usury do not stand except as one whom Satan prostrates by touch} up to His Almighty saying {then for you are your capitals, you wrong not nor are wronged} 450 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the verses at the end of Surah al-Baqarah concerning usury were revealed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and recited them to the people, then he forbade trading in wine."
"451","451 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: When the verses at the end of Surah al-Baqarah concerning usury were revealed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out and forbade trading in wine."
"452","His Almighty saying: {And if the debtor is in hardship, then respite till ease} 452 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, he said: ( ) narrated to me, from al-Rabi' ibn Khuthaym, that he had a debt owed by a man, and he would say: So-and-so has sinned; if you are well-off then pay it, and if you are in hardship then till ease. So I mentioned that to Ibrahim, and he said: That is only concerning usury. [Flag: one narrator name is left blank in the source]"
"453","453 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Hisham narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin, that two men disputed before Shurayh concerning a right owed by one to the other, so Shurayh ruled against him and ordered his imprisonment, whereupon a man present said: He is insolvent, and Allah Almighty says in His Book: {And if the debtor is in hardship, then respite till ease} he said: That is concerning usury, and Allah says: {Allah commands you to render trusts to their owners}."
"454","454 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, regarding the Almighty saying {And if the debtor is in hardship, then respite till ease} - he said: That is concerning usury."
"455","His Almighty saying: {And call to witness two witnesses from your men, and if there are not two men, then a man and two women from those you approve of witnesses} 455 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, he said: I wrote to Ibn Abbas asking him about the testimony of children, so he wrote to me: Allah Almighty says: {from those you approve of witnesses}, and they are not among those we approve; it is not permissible."
"456","456 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Sufyan al-Thawri, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, regarding the Almighty saying {and call to witness two witnesses from your men} - he said: From freemen."
"457","457 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Dawud ibn Abi Hind narrated to us, he said: I asked Mujahid about zihar from a slave-girl, and he said: It is nothing. I said: Does not Allah say: {And those who do zihar from their wives} - are they not women? He said: And Allah says: {And call to witness two witnesses from your men} - then is the testimony of slaves permissible?"
"458","His Almighty saying: {And let not the witnesses refuse when they are called} 458 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Muhammad ibn Thabit al-Abdi narrated to us, he said: A man asked Ata ibn Abi Rabah, while I was present, about His saying {and let not the witnesses refuse when they are called}: is it before they are taken as witnesses or after they have been made witnesses? He said: No, rather after they have witnessed."
"459","459 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Amir al-Muzani informed me, he said: I heard Ata saying: Concerning establishing the testimony."
"460","460 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Ikrimah, he said: Concerning establishing the testimony."
"461","461 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sharik narrated to us, from Salim al-Aftas, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: The one who has already been made a witness, not the one who has not witnessed."
"462","462 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, he said: Ibn Abi Najih narrated to us, from Mujahid, he said: When you have a testimony and are called."
"463","463 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym and Khalid and Isma'il narrated to us, from Yunus ibn Ubayd, from al-Hasan, he said: When he is called to bear witness, and when he is called to establish it - both."
"464","464 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, he said: I said to Ibrahim: I am called to testify while I am forgetful? He said: Then do not testify if you have forgotten."
"465","465 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: I said: I am called to testify while I am unwilling? He said: Then do not testify if you wish."
"466","His Almighty saying: {And let no scribe or witness be harmed} 466 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, he said: Umar used to recite: {And let no scribe or witness be harmed}."
"467","His Almighty saying: {And if you are on a journey and find no scribe, then pledges taken} 467 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that he recited: (And if you do not find a writing), and said: A writing may exist while the inkwell or the sheet is not found."
"468","468 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yazid narrated to us, from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite: (And if you do not find scribes), he said: Meaning the scribe, the sheet, the inkwell, and the pen."
"469","469 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Khirrit, from Ikrimah, regarding the Almighty saying: (And if you do not find scribes) - and he said: What if they find a scribe but do not find the sheet and the inkwell?"
"470","470 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, that he used to recite: {Then pledges taken}."
"471","471 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim: Pledges - {then pledges taken}."
"472","472 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Rashid informed me, from al-Hasan, and Abu al-Ashhab, from Abu al-Raja, that they both used to recite: {Then pledges taken}."
"473","His Almighty saying: {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it} up to His Almighty saying: {So help us against the disbelieving people} 473 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, regarding the Almighty saying {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it} - he said: It was revealed concerning testimony. [Flag: the name Sa'id is repeated twice in the chain in the source]"
"474","474 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Attab ibn Bashir narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Mujahid, regarding the Almighty saying {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it} - they said: That weighed heavily upon them; they said: O Messenger of Allah, we speak to ourselves things we would not like any creature to know, and shall we have such-and-such? He said: Have you indeed encountered this? That is pure faith. Then Allah Almighty revealed: {The Messenger believes in what was sent down to him from his Lord}, the two verses."
"475","475 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever recites in a night the two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah, they suffice him."
"476","476 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abu Mas'ud al-Ansari, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The two verses from the end of Surah al-Baqarah - whoever recites them in a night, they suffice him."
"477","477 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Khalid narrated to us, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite: {Each believed in Allah and His angels and His Book} (singular: His Book)."
"478","478 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Bayan, from Hakim ibn Jabir, he said: Jibril said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah has praised you and your nation well when {The Messenger believes in what was sent down to him from his Lord and the believers, each believes in Allah} was revealed - so ask, you will be given. So he asked {Allah burdens no soul except its capacity} until he sealed the surah with the asking of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"479","479 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Bayan, from Amir al-Sha'bi, he said: This verse {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it} was abrogated by what came after it: {For it what it earned, and against it what it acquired}."
"480","480 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sayyar narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it} was revealed, it contained severity, then this verse after it came down and abrogated it: {For it what it earned, and against it what it acquired}."
"481","481 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir narrated to us, from al-Dahhak, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, regarding the Almighty saying {And whether you reveal what is in yourselves or conceal it, Allah will call you to account for it, then He forgives whom He wills and punishes whom He wills} - she said: It is the man who intends disobedience but does not do it, then grief and sorrow are sent upon him equal to what he had intended of disobedience - that is his reckoning."
"482","482 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir narrated to us, from al-Dahhak, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: The verse after it abrogated it: {For it what it earned, and against it what it acquired}."
"483","483 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Salamah ibn Nubayt, he said: I heard al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim saying: Jibril brought it, and with him angels as Allah Almighty willed: {The Messenger believes in what was sent down to him from his Lord} up to His saying {Our Lord, take us not to task if we forget} - He said: That is yours; {or err} He said: That is yours; {Our Lord, lay not upon us a burden as You laid upon those before us} He said: That is yours; {and burden us not with what we have no strength for} He said: That is yours; {and pardon us} He said: That is yours; {and forgive us} He said: That is yours; {and have mercy on us} He said: That is yours; {You are our Master, so help us against the disbelieving people} He said: That is yours."
"484","484 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Awn ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: I heard al-Mughirah ibn Abd al-Malik al-Qurashi saying: It was said: Learn Surah al-Baqarah, for its taking is a merit and its leaving is a regret, and the idle sorcerers (al-batalah) cannot bear it. Learn the two radiant ones: al-Baqarah and Al Imran."
"485","485 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Marwan ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, he said: Wiqa ibn Iyas al-Asadi narrated to us, he said: Sa'id ibn Jubayr heard me one night while I was reciting al-Baqarah, Al Imran, and al-Nisa. He said: Did I not hear you last night reciting al-Baqarah, al-Nisa, and Al Imran? I said: Yes. He said: Do not do so; keep to the Ha-Mim surahs and al-Mufassal. Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Whoever recites al-Baqarah, al-Nisa, and Al Imran is recorded with Allah among the wise."
"486","Chapter of the exegesis of Surah Al Imran. His Almighty saying: {Allah, there is no god but He, the Living, the Sustainer} 486 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from his father, that Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, used to recite: (The Living, the Ever-Abiding)."
"487","487 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: al-Hakam ibn Zuhayr narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Amr ibn Maymun, may Allah be pleased with him, he said: I heard Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, reciting: (The Living, the Ever-Abiding)."
"488","488 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, that he used to recite: (The Living, the Ever-Abiding)."
"489","489 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq al-Kufi narrated to us, from Abu Khalid al-Kinani, from Ibn Mas'ud, that he used to recite it likewise."
"490","490 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that he used to recite: {The Living, the Sustainer}."
"491","491 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ashhab informed me, from Abu Raja al-Utaridi, that he used to recite likewise."
"492","His Almighty saying: {It is He who sent down upon you the Book; in it are decisive verses, they are the Mother of the Book, and others unspecific. As for those in whose hearts is deviation, they follow what is unspecific of it, seeking discord and seeking its interpretation; and none knows its interpretation but Allah. And those firm in knowledge say: We believe in it, all is from our Lord; and none remembers but those of understanding} 492 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Yahya al-Abahh narrated to us, he said: Abd Allah ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated to us, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited: {It is He who sent down upon you the Book; in it are decisive verses, they are the Mother of the Book, and others unspecific. As for those in whose hearts is deviation} up to His saying {and none remembers but those of understanding}. He said: When you see those who dispute about it, they are they - so beware of them."
"493","493 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hudayj ibn Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abd Allah ibn Qays, from Ibn Abbas: {In it are decisive verses, they are the Mother of the Book} - he said: Three verses from Surah al-An'am: {Say: Come, I will recite what your Lord forbade you}."
"494","His Almighty saying: {And Allah knows best what she delivered} 494 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isa ibn Yunus narrated to us, he said: al-A'mash informed us, from Umarah ibn Umayr, he said: We were sitting with Yahya ibn Watthab when we mentioned this verse {And Allah knows best what she delivered}, and al-Aswad ibn Yazid came in a waist-wrapper, cloak, and turban, and stood praying in his wrapper, cloak, and sandals. We said: Which of you will go to him and ask him? Yahya said: I will. So he went to him and asked him, then came back to us and said: {And Allah knows best what she delivered}. [Flag: the answer reported is only the verse wording; the question itself is not stated in the source]"
"495","495 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya and Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Umarah ibn Umayr, from al-Aswad, that he used to recite: {And Allah knows best what she delivered}."
"496","496 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from one who narrated to him, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite: {And Allah knows best what she delivered}. [Flag: one link in the chain is unnamed in the source]"
"497","497 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah and Hushaym narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that he used to recite: {with what she delivered}, with raf' (nominative). [Flag: remark after the reading is truncated in the source]"
"498","His Almighty saying: {And Zakariyya took charge of her} 498 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, he said: I said to al-A'mash: Humayd recites {O Zakariyya} with jazm (pausal form) - and it pleased him."
"499","His Almighty saying: {And I inform you of what you eat and what you store in your houses} 499 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Salim narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, regarding the Almighty saying {And I inform you of what you eat and what you store in your houses} - he said: Isa son of Maryam would say to the boy in school: Your family has hidden for you such-and-such; that is His saying {and what you store}."
"500","His Almighty saying: {The likeness of Isa with Allah is as the likeness of Adam; He created him from dust, then said to him: Be, and he is. The truth from your Lord, so be not of the doubters. Then whoever disputes with you about it after what came to you of knowledge, say: Come, let us call our sons and your sons, our women and your women, ourselves and yourselves, then let us invoke and place the curse of Allah upon the liars} 500 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) offered mutual imprecation (mula'anah) to the people of Najran, al-Sayyid and al-Aqib accepted that from him, then they returned to a distinguished man among them and he said to them: You have done nothing; by Allah, if he is a prophet, Allah will not disobey Himself against you (?), and if he is a king he will subjugate you. They said to him: What do you advise? He said: I advise that you go in the morning, for he will come in the morning for your appointment; when he comes to you in the morning, he will offer you mutual imprecation; when he offers that to you, say to him: We seek refuge with Allah. So they went in the morning, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came in the morning holding Hasan's hand, with Husayn following him and Fatimah walking behind him. He said to them: Will you take up the matter you set out upon, of mutual imprecation? They said: We seek refuge with Allah. He repeated that to them, and they said: We seek refuge with Allah - twice or thrice. He said to them: Will you accept Islam, that you enter Islam and have what the Muslims have and bear what the Muslims bear? They did not accept that and disliked it. He said to them: Will you accept paying the jizyah, which you pay while you are humbled, as Allah Almighty said? They accepted that and said: We have no strength for war with the Arabs."
"501","His Almighty saying: {The worthiest of people of Ibrahim are those who followed him, and this Prophet, and those who believe; and Allah is the Guardian of the believers} 501 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Abu al-Duha, from Masruq, from Ibn Mas'ud, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Every prophet has guardians among the believers, and my guardian among them is my father and the intimate friend of my Lord. Then he recited: {The worthiest of people of Ibrahim are those who followed him, and this Prophet}."
"502","His Almighty saying: {And a party of the People of the Book said: Believe in what was sent down upon those who believe at the start of the day and disbelieve at its end, that they may return} 502 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Husayn, from Abu Malik, regarding the Almighty saying {Believe in what was sent down upon those who believe at the start of the day and disbelieve at its end} - he said: The Jews said: Believe along with them in what they say at the start of the day, and apostatize at its end, that they may return along with you."
"503","His Almighty saying: {Those who purchase with Allah's covenant and their oaths a small price - they shall have no share in the Hereafter, and Allah will not speak to them nor look at them on the Day of Resurrection nor purify them, and for them is a painful punishment} 503 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Shaqiq, from Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever swears a wicked oath to cut off thereby the wealth of a Muslim man will meet Allah Almighty while He is angry with him. Al-Ash'ath said: By Allah, that was concerning me; between me and a Jewish man there was land, and he denied me my right, so I brought him before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Have you proof? I said: No. So he said to the Jew: Swear. I said: O Messenger of Allah, then he will swear and take my wealth. So Allah Almighty revealed: {Those who purchase with Allah's covenant and their oaths a small price} to the end of the verse."
"504","His Almighty saying: {But be godly scholars, by what you taught of the Book and by what you studied} 504 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Abu Razin, regarding the Almighty saying {Be godly scholars} - he said: Understanding jurists, learned men."
"505","His Almighty saying: {Do they seek other than Allah's religion, while to Him submitted whoever is in the heavens and the earth, willingly or unwillingly, and to Him they are returned} 505 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, he said: When Tawus was asked about a man preferring some of his children, he recited: {Do they seek the judgment of ignorance?}. [Flag: the verse he recited differs from the heading verse in the source]"
"506","His Almighty saying: {And whoever seeks other than Islam as religion, it shall never be accepted from him, and in the Hereafter he is of the losers} 506 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: From Sufyan, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Ikrimah, he said: When {And whoever seeks other than Islam as religion, it shall never be accepted from him} was revealed, the Jews said: Then we are Muslims. So Allah Almighty said: Refute them with their own proof, and say: Allah obligated upon the Muslims the pilgrimage to the House for whoever can find a way to it, and whoever disbelieves among the religious communities - then Allah is Free of need of the worlds."
"507","His Almighty saying: {You shall never attain righteousness until you spend of what you love} 507 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, he said: When {You shall never attain righteousness until you spend of what you love} was revealed, Zayd ibn Harithah came with a horse called Sabal (?), and said: O Messenger of Allah, this is in Allah's path? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has accepted it from you. [Flag: the horse's name is uncertain in the source]"
"508","His Almighty saying: {All food was lawful to the Children of Israel except what Israel forbade himself before the Torah was sent down} 508 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Yusuf ibn Mahak, that a bedouin said to Ibn Abbas: I said to my wife: She is haram upon me? He said: She is not haram upon you. He said: Then where is Allah Almighty's saying {All food was lawful to the Children of Israel except what Israel forbade himself}? He said: Do you know what Israel forbade himself? He said: No. He said: Israel was seized by sciatica (al-ansa) and it emaciated him, so he vowed to Allah that if Allah cured him he would never eat a vein; that is why the Jews remove the veins and do not eat them."
"509","His Almighty saying: {The first House laid down for people is that at Bakkah, blessed and a guidance for the worlds. In it are clear signs: the Station of Ibrahim, and whoever enters it is safe} 509 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Mughirah, from Ibrahim, he said: Bakkah is the site of the House, and Makkah is the rest of the town."
"510","510 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Muslim ibn Khalid al-Zanji narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid and Ata, they said: The Station of Ibrahim is the Sacred Mosque, Mina, Arafah, and Muzdalifah."
"511","511 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Isma'il ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Hammad, he said: I asked Sa'id ibn Jubayr: Why was it named Bakkah? He said: Because men and women crowd together therein."
"512","512 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Malik and Hajjaj narrated to us, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, that he used to recite: In it is a clear sign: the Station of Ibrahim (singular)."
"513","513 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, the like thereof."
"514","514 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Salamah ibn Kuhayl, he said: I heard Mujahid saying: It was named Bakkah only because people press one another therein."
"515","His Almighty saying: {And to Allah is owed by people the pilgrimage to the House, for whoever can find a way to it; and whoever disbelieves - then Allah is Free of need of the worlds} 515 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Juwaybir narrated to us, from al-Dahhak, he said: When this verse {And to Allah is owed by people the pilgrimage to the House} was revealed, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered the religious communities: the Arab polytheists, the Christians, the Jews, the Magians, and the Sabians, and said: Allah Almighty has obligated the Hajj upon you, so perform Hajj to the House. None accepted it but the Muslims; then they disbelieved in the House - and that is the Almighty saying {and whoever disbelieves}, meaning whoever denies, {then Allah is Free of need of the worlds}."
"516","516 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, regarding the Almighty saying {and whoever disbelieves - then Allah is Free of need of the worlds}: Whoever, if he performs Hajj, does not deem it righteousness, and if he leaves it, does not deem it sin."
"517","517 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Hisham, from al-Hasan, he said: Whoever does not deem it obligatory."
"518","518 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym and Khalid narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked: What is the means to Hajj? He said: Provision and a mount."
"519","His Almighty saying: {And hold fast all together to Allah's rope and be not divided} 519 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Jami' ibn Abi Rashid, from Abu Wa'il, from Abd Allah, regarding His saying {And hold fast all together to Allah's rope} - he said: Allah's rope is the Qur'an."
"520","520 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: al-Awwam narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Mas'ud, he said: Allah's rope is the community (al-jama'ah)."
"521","His Almighty saying: {And let there be of you a nation calling to good and commanding right and forbidding wrong, and they are the successful} 521 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, that he heard Ibn al-Zubayr saying {And let there be of you a nation calling to good and commanding right and forbidding wrong} and seeking Allah's help against what befalls them - and I do not know whether it was his recitation or his explanation. [Flag: uncertain whether the added words are recitation or exegesis]"
"522","His Almighty saying: {The likeness of what they spend in this worldly life is as the likeness of a wind wherein is intense cold, which struck the tillage of a people who wronged themselves and destroyed it} 522 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Abu Humayd al-Ru'asi, from Antarah, from Ibn Abbas, regarding the Almighty saying {a wind wherein is intense cold} - he said: Frost."
"523","His Almighty saying: {When two parties of you were about to lose heart, while Allah was their Guardian; and upon Allah let the believers rely} 523 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, that he heard Jabir ibn Abd Allah saying: It was revealed concerning us - concerning Banu Salimah and Banu Harithah: {When two parties of you were about to lose heart, while Allah was their Guardian} - and it would not please me if it had not been revealed."
"524","His Almighty saying: {Your Lord reinforcing you with five thousand angels, marked} 524 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak, regarding the Almighty saying {Your Lord reinforcing you with five thousand angels, marked} - he said: Marked with white wool."
"525","His Almighty saying: {And those who, when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves, remember Allah and seek forgiveness for their sins - and who forgives sins but Allah - and do not persist in what they did, while they know} 525 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, regarding His saying {And those who, when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves} - he said: Two sins they committed: indecency is a sin, and wronging themselves is a sin."
"526","526 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Alqamah and al-Aswad, they said: Abd Allah said: In Allah's Book there are two verses: no servant commits a sin then recites them and seeks forgiveness from Allah Almighty but He forgives him: {And those who, when they commit an indecency or wrong themselves, remember Allah and seek forgiveness for their sins - and who forgives sins but Allah} and His saying {And whoever does evil or wrongs himself then seeks Allah's forgiveness will find Allah Forgiving, Merciful}."
"527","His Almighty saying: {This is a clarification for people, and guidance and admonition for the godfearing} 527 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Bayan, from Amir al-Sha'bi, regarding the Almighty saying {This is a clarification for people, and guidance and admonition for the godfearing} - he said: A clarification from blindness, guidance from error, and admonition from ignorance."
"528","His Almighty saying: {And how many a prophet fought, with him many godly men; they did not weaken for what befell them in Allah's path, nor became weak, nor humbled themselves; and Allah loves the patient} 528 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Attab ibn Bashir narrated to us, he said: Khusayf narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Abi Maryam and Abu Ubaydah, from Ibn Mas'ud, regarding the Almighty saying {And how many a prophet fought, with him many godly men} - he says: Fought; do you not see that He says {they did not weaken for what befell them in Allah's path} - the verse."
"529","529 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Attab narrated to us, from Khusayf, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, that he used to say: We never heard that any prophet was killed in fighting."
"530","530 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, and I am Awf, from Ibrahim, that they both used to recite: {fought with him}. [Flag: the chain wording is tangled in the source]"
"531","531 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, regarding the Almighty saying {fought with him many godly men} - he said: Jurists, learned men. He said: And Ibn Abbas said: They are the numerous multitudes."
"532","532 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ikrimah, he said: The numerous multitudes."
"533","533 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated to us, from al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim, regarding the Almighty saying {fought with him many godly men} - he said: One ribbah is a thousand."
"534","His Almighty saying: {So pardon them, seek forgiveness for them, and consult them in the matter} 534 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Shubrumah, from al-Hasan, regarding the Almighty saying {and consult them in the matter} - he said: Allah knew he has no need of them, but He wanted those after him to follow his practice."
"535","535 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from a man, from Amr, from Ibn Abbas, that he recited: (And consult them in part of the matter). [Flag: one link in the chain is unnamed in the source]"
"536","His Almighty saying: {And it is not for a prophet to embezzle} 536 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us: Mughirah informed us, from Ibrahim, and Awf informed us, from al-Hasan, that they both used to recite: {And it is not for a prophet to embezzle}."
"537","537 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Awf narrated to us, from al-Hasan, he said: That he be betrayed."
"538","His Almighty saying: {And think not those killed in Allah's path dead; rather alive with their Lord, provided for. Rejoicing in what Allah gave them of His bounty, and rejoicing for those who have not yet joined them from behind them - no fear upon them nor do they grieve} 538 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Sa'id ibn Masruq, from Abu al-Duha, he said: It was revealed concerning the slain of Uhud: {And think not those killed in Allah's path dead; rather alive with their Lord, provided for}. And there was revealed concerning them {and that He take martyrs from you}. Seventy men of them were killed: four of the Emigrants - Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib of Banu Hashim, Mus'ab ibn Umayr of Banu Abd al-Dar, al-Shammas ibn Uthman of Banu Makhzum, and Abd Allah ibn Jahsh of Banu Asad ibn Khuzaymah - and the rest of them of the Ansar."
"539","539 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, he said: Abd Allah ibn Murrah narrated to us, from Masruq, he said: Abd Allah was asked about the Almighty saying {And think not those killed in Allah's path dead; rather alive with their Lord} and he said: We have indeed asked about that, and he said: Their souls are as green birds roaming in Paradise wherever they wish, then sheltering in lamps hung from the Throne. While they are thus, your Lord Almighty looked upon them with a look and said to them: Ask Me whatever you wish. They said: Our Lord, what shall we ask You while we are in Paradise roaming wherever we wish? While they are thus, your Lord Almighty looked upon them with a look and said to them: Ask Me whatever you wish. They said: Our Lord, what shall we ask You while we are in Paradise roaming wherever we wish? When they saw they would not be left unless they ask, they said: We ask You to return our souls into our bodies in the world so we may be killed in Your path. When He saw they ask only this, they were left."
"540","540 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ali al-Sulami, from Abd Allah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abd Allah, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Know that Allah Almighty gave life to your father and said to him: Wish. So he wished to return to the world and be killed another time. He said: I have decreed that they shall not return."
"541","His Almighty saying: {Those who answered Allah and the Messenger after injury befell them} 541 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, from Ibn Mas'ud, that he used to recite: {after injury (al-qarh) befell them}."
"542","542 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abbad ibn Rashid narrated to us, from al-Hasan, and Abu al-Ashhab, from Abu Raja, that they both used to recite: {after injury befell them}."
"543","His Almighty saying: {Those to whom people said: People have gathered against you, so fear them - but it increased them in faith, and they said: Allah suffices us, and excellent is the Trustee. So they returned with blessing from Allah and bounty, no harm touching them} 543 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, he said: Badr was a market in the pre-Islamic age, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had promised Abu Sufyan to meet him there. A man met them and said to them: There is a great host of polytheists there. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called the people forth; they came to Badr and met no one. The coward turned back and the bold went on; they traded there and met no one. Then there was revealed: {Those to whom people said: People have gathered against you, so fear them - but it increased them in faith, and they said: Allah suffices us, and excellent is the Trustee. So they returned with blessing from Allah and bounty}."
"544","544 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Mutarrif, from Atiyyah al-Awfi, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How can I enjoy pleasure when the Bearer of the Horn has taken the Horn, bent his forehead, and inclined his hearing, waiting when he will be commanded to blow into the Trumpet? They said: Then what do you command us, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Say: Allah suffices us, and excellent is the Trustee; we rely upon Allah."
"545","545 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, he said: A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Your two fathers were indeed of those who answered Allah and the Messenger."
"546","His Almighty saying: {And let not those who disbelieve think that Our respite to them is good for them; We respite them only that they increase in sin, and for them is a humiliating punishment} 546 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Ma'shar narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b, he said: Death is good for the believer and the disbeliever. Then he recited: {And let not those who disbelieve think that Our respite to them is good for them; We respite them only that they increase in sin} and {And what is with Allah is better for the righteous}. Then he said: The disbeliever - the longer he lives, the severer his punishment on the Day of Resurrection."
"547","547 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Faraj ibn Fadalah narrated to us, from Luqman ibn Amir, from Abu al-Darda, he said: There is no believer but death is good for him, and no disbeliever but death is good for him. Whoever does not believe me - Allah Almighty says: {And what is with Allah is better for the righteous} and {And let not those who disbelieve think that Our respite to them is good for them; We respite them only that they increase in sin, and for them is a humiliating punishment}."
"548","His Almighty saying: {Allah would not leave the believers as you are until He distinguishes the corrupt from the good} 548 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: al-Harith ibn Ubayd narrated to us, from Malik ibn Dinar, that he recited: {until He distinguishes the corrupt from the good}."
"549","His Almighty saying: {And let not those who are miserly with what Allah gave them of His bounty think it good for them; rather it is evil for them. They shall be collared with what they were miserly with on the Day of Resurrection} 549 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu Wa'il, from Ibn Mas'ud, regarding the Almighty saying {They shall be collared with what they were miserly with on the Day of Resurrection} - he said: He is collared with a bald serpent with two spots in its mouth, pecking his head, and he says: What have I to do with you? It says: I am your wealth with which you were miserly."
"550","550 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, he said: Abu Hashim narrated to us, from Abu Wa'il, from Masruq, he said: It is the man whom Allah provides with wealth, then he withholds from his kin the right Allah made for them in his wealth; so it is made a snake and he is collared with it, and he says to the snake: What have I to do with you? It says: I am your wealth."
"551","551 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, regarding the Almighty saying {They shall be collared with what they were miserly with on the Day of Resurrection} - he said: A collar of fire."
"552","His Almighty saying: {So their Lord answered them: I waste not the work of any worker of you, male or female, some of you from others. Those who emigrated and were expelled from their homes and were harmed in My path and fought and were killed - I shall expiate from them their evils and admit them to Gardens beneath which rivers flow, a reward from Allah; and with Allah is the best reward} 552 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Salamah, a descendant of Umm Salamah, he said: Umm Salamah said: O Messenger of Allah, do I not hear Allah Almighty mentioning women at all concerning emigration? So Allah Almighty revealed: {So their Lord answered them: I waste not the work of any worker of you, male or female} to the end of the verse. He said: The Ansar said: She is the first woman traveler who came to us."
"553","553 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Abu Attaf, he said: The name of Al Imran in the Torah is Taybah. [Flag: reading uncertain in the source]"
"554","Exegesis of Surah al-Nisa. His Almighty saying: {And if you fear you cannot do justice to the orphans, then marry what pleases you of women, two, three, and four; but if you fear you cannot be just, then one, or what your right hands possess; that is nearer that you not deviate} 554 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr, he said: I heard him saying: Allah sent Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while people were upon the pre-Islamic order, except that they would be commanded something and forbidden something. They would ask about the orphans, while women had no number and no mention. So Allah Almighty revealed: {And if you fear you cannot do justice to the orphans, then marry what pleases you of women, two, three, and four}. A man would marry as many as he wished. He said: Just as you fear you cannot do justice to the orphans, so fear regarding women that you not be just to them."
"555","555 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, regarding the Almighty saying {that is nearer that you not deviate} - he said: That you not incline."
"556","556 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Husayn narrated to us, from Abu Malik, regarding the Almighty saying {that is nearer that you not deviate} - he said: That you not act unjustly."
"557","557 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Dawud narrated to us, from Ikrimah, he said: I heard him saying regarding the Almighty saying {that is nearer that you not deviate}: That is, that you not incline. Then he recited to me a verse composed by Abu Talib. [Flag: the verse itself is not quoted in the source]"
"558","558 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Bayan, from Amir al-Sha'bi, regarding the Almighty saying {that is nearer that you not deviate} - he said: That you not incline; I think he said: From Ibn Abbas."
"559","His Almighty saying: {But if they willingly give you of it anything, then consume it wholesome, pleasant} 559 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Sayyar narrated to us, from Abu Salih, regarding the Almighty saying {But if they willingly give you of it anything, then consume it wholesome, pleasant} - he said: When a husband married off his unmarried ward he would take her dowry, so they were forbidden that."
"560","560 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Ubaydah informed me, he said: Ibrahim said to me: Have you eaten of the wholesome pleasant? He said: And what is that? He said: Of your wife's dower."
"561","His Almighty saying: {And give not the fools your wealth which Allah made for you a support} 561 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Abu Hurrah narrated to us, and Yunus informed us, from al-Hasan, regarding the Almighty saying {And give not the fools your wealth} - he said: The fools are the minors, and women are of the fools."
"562","562 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Awn ibn Musa narrated to us, he said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah saying: Visit the women often (?), for she is foolish; if you obey her she will destroy you. [Flag: opening phrase obscure in the source]"
"563","His Almighty saying: {And test the orphans until when they reach marriageable age; then if you perceive in them maturity, deliver to them their wealth} 563 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Mujahid, he said: The orphan's wealth is not delivered to him, even if he grows gray-haired, until maturity is perceived in him. [Flag: end of the report garbled in the source]"
"564","564 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mughirah, from al-Sha'bi, he said: A man may grow gray-haired while no maturity is perceived in him."
"565","565 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Mansur, from Ibrahim, regarding the Almighty saying {then if you perceive in them maturity} - he said: Intellect."
"566","His Almighty saying: {And whoever is rich, let him abstain; and whoever is poor, let him consume with fairness} 566 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr, from Ata and Ikrimah, regarding the Almighty saying {and whoever is poor, let him consume with fairness} - they said: He puts his hand (eating together with them)."
"567","567 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, he said: He takes it as a loan; when he becomes able, he repays it."
"568","568 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mughirah narrated to us, from Ibrahim, he said: Fairness is not wearing linen or robes; rather, what fills hunger and covers nakedness."
"569","569 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Ya'qub ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, he said: If the orphan's guardian eats in return for his maintenance of the orphan, with fairness. [Flag: wording obscure in the source]"
"570","570 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Jarir narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq al-Shaybani, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, he said: The executor puts his hand along with their hands, and does not wear the turban and what is above it. [Flag: last clause obscure in the source]"
"571","571 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, from Ibn Abbas, that a man asked him, saying: An orphan is in my care - may I drink of the milk? He said: If you return its stray camel, repair its basin, and treat its mange, then drink without harming offspring nor exhausting in milking."
"572","572 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hammad ibn Zayd and Sufyan narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan al-Urani, that a man said: O Messenger of Allah, with what may I strike my orphan? He said: With what you would strike your child. He said: May I take from his wealth? He said: Without accumulating wealth nor shielding your wealth with his wealth."
"573","573 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Mansur narrated to us, from al-Hasan, that he used to say concerning the orphan's guardian: He said: If he is needy, he puts his hand along with the orphan's hand."
"574","574 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Salamah ibn Alqamah narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin, he said: I asked Abidah about the Almighty saying {and whoever is poor, let him consume with fairness} - he said: It is a loan. Do you not see His Almighty saying {Then when you deliver to them their wealth, call witnesses over them}?"
"575","575 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Hajjaj narrated to us, from one who heard Sa'id ibn Jubayr and Mujahid saying that. [Flag: the direct hearer is unnamed in the source]"
"576","His Almighty saying: {And when the division is attended by kinsmen, orphans, and the needy, provide for them of it and speak to them fair words} 576 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Bishr, from Sa'id ibn Jubayr: People say this verse has been abrogated: {And when the division is attended by kinsmen, orphans, and the needy, provide for them of it}. He said: No, by Allah, it has not been abrogated, but people have neglected it. There are two guardians: a guardian who inherits - he is the one who provides; and a guardian who does not inherit - he is the one who speaks fair words: It is the wealth of orphans and I have nothing in it."
"577","577 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from Mujahid, regarding the Almighty saying {so provide for them of it} - he said: An obligatory right from what souls give willingly."
"578","578 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Mansur narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Yahya ibn Ya'mar, he said: Three Madinan firm verses many people neglected: the Almighty saying {And when the division is attended by kinsmen, orphans, and the needy, provide for them of it and speak to them fair words}, and the verse of seeking permission {And those of you who have not reached puberty}, and His saying {We created you from male and female and made you peoples and tribes that you may know one another; the noblest of you with Allah is the most godfearing}."
"579","579 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Khalid ibn Abd Allah narrated to us, from Yunus, from al-Hasan, regarding the Almighty saying {And when the division is attended by kinsmen, orphans, and the needy, provide for them of it} - he said: One other than the deceased's kin is given a small portion, a cupful or something. And he used to say of it: It has not been abrogated."
"580","580 - Sa'id narrated to us, he said: Hushaym narrated to us, he said: Yunus and Mansur narrated to us, from al-Hasan, and Mughirah, from Ibrahim, that they both said: It is firm and not abrogated."
"581","581 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from Awf, from Ibn Sirin, he said: They used to give them a small gift when any of them attended the division."
"582","582 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from Juwaybir, from al-Dahhak, concerning His statement, mighty and majestic: 'so provide for them from it' - he said: It is abrogated by inheritance."
"583","583 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Mughira narrated, from al-Sha'bi, he said: A man said: I will revive today a verse from the Book of Allah, mighty and majestic, even if from my own share."
"584","His saying, exalted: 'And let those fear who, if they left behind weak offspring, would fear for them, so let them fear Allah and speak a right word.' 584 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated, from Mujahid, concerning His statement: 'And let those fear...' - he said: When a man attended a dying person, he would say to him: bequeath to so-and-so, bequeath to so-and-so, do this and do that, until that harmed his heirs; so Allah said: 'And let those fear...' He said: Let them look after the heirs of this one as each of them looks after his own heirs, so let them fear Allah and command him with justice and truth."
"585","585 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abu Shihab narrated, from al-A'mash, from Muslim, from Masruq, that he attended a man making a will, and he favored some heirs over others, so he said to him: Allah has divided among you and divided well; whoever prefers his own opinion over the opinion of Allah, exalted, goes astray; so bequeath to a relative who does not inherit, then leave the wealth as Allah divided it."
"586","His saying, exalted: 'Indeed those who consume the wealth of orphans unjustly only consume fire in their bellies, and they will burn in a Blaze.' 586 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Abu Ishaq narrated, from Ikrima, he said: When 'Indeed those who consume the wealth of orphans...' was revealed, people isolated their orphans and would not mix with them, so they complained of that to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); then 'and if you mix with them, they are your brothers, and Allah knows the corrupter from the reformer' was revealed, so they mixed with them again."
"587","His saying, exalted: 'And if a man or a woman is inherited as kalala and he has a brother or a sister, then for each of them a sixth, and if they are more than that they share in a third.' 587 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Amr, from Tawus, he said: Umar ordered Hafsa to ask the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about kalala; she delayed him until he had put on his clothes, then she asked him about it, and he dictated it to her and said: Who ordered you with this, Umar? I do not think they will understand it; did the summer verse not suffice them? Sufyan said: 'And if a man is inherited as kalala' - and he did not understand it, and he said: O Allah, whoever understands it, for I did not understand it."
"588","588 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Amr ibn Dinar, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad, he said: I asked Ibn Abbas about kalala; he said: It is what excludes the child and the parent. I said to him: 'If a man perishes and has no child'? So he became angry and rebuked me."
"589","589 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Sulayman al-Ahwal, from Tawus, he heard Ibn Abbas saying: I was the last of the people to part from Umar, and I heard him saying: The statement is what you said. I said: And what did I say? He said: Kalala is one who has no child."
"590","590 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from Zakariya, from Abu Ishaq, from Sulaym ibn Abdillah, he said: I heard Ibn Abbas saying: Kalala is what excludes the parent and the child."
"591","591 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sufyan narrated, from Asim al-Ahwal, from al-Sha'bi, he said: Umar said: Kalala is what excludes the child; and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Kalala is what excludes the child and the parent; then when Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was stabbed, he said: I am ashamed before Allah, mighty and majestic, to oppose Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him: kalala is what excludes the child and the parent."
"592","592 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, from Ya'la ibn Ata, from al-Qasim ibn Rabi'a ibn Qanif, from Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas, that he used to recite: 'and he has a brother or a sister from the mother.'"
"593","593 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated, he said: Abu Hayyan al-Taymi narrated, from al-Sha'bi, from Ibn Umar, he said: I heard Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, saying on the pulpit of Madina: O people, the prohibition of wine came down on the day it came down, and it is from five things: from grapes, dates, honey, wheat, and barley; and wine is whatever intoxicates the mind; and three things, O people, I wished the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had not parted from us until he had covenanted to us a covenant we would hold to: the grandfather, kalala, and doors of usury."
"594","His saying, exalted: 'And those of your women who commit indecency, bring four witnesses against them from among you; if they testify, confine them in houses until death takes them or Allah makes for them a way.' 594 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Hushaym narrated, he said: Mansur narrated, from al-Hasan, he said: Hittan ibn Abdillah al-Raqashi narrated, from Ubada ibn al-Samit, he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Take from me, for Allah has made for them a way: the virgin with the virgin, one hundred lashes and exile for a year; and the previously married with the previously married, one hundred lashes then stoning."
"595","595 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Sharik narrated, from Firas, from al-Sha'bi, from Masruq, from Ubayy ibn Ka'b, he said: Two virgins, when they fornicate, are flogged and exiled; and two previously married are stoned; and two old persons are flogged and stoned."
"596","His saying, exalted: 'Repentance is only upon Allah for those who do evil in ignorance then repent soon; those, Allah turns to them, and Allah is Knowing, Wise. And repentance is not for those who do evil deeds until death comes to one of them and he says: I repent now, nor those who die as disbelievers; those, We have prepared for them a painful punishment.' 596 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Zakariya narrated, from a shaykh of the people of Kufa, he said: I heard al-Dahhak ibn Muzahim saying concerning His saying: 'they repent soon' - he said: Every repentance before death is 'soon.'"
"597","597 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Baylamani, from a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), that he heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: By the One in Whose Hand is my soul, there is no man who repents a day before he dies but Allah, mighty and majestic, accepts his repentance. He said: So I told that to a man from the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Did you hear that from him? I said: Yes. He said: Then I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no man who repents half a day before he dies but Allah accepts his repentance. He said: So I told that to a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Did you hear that from him? I said: Yes. He said: Then I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no man who repents before he dies by a forenoon but Allah accepts his repentance. Then I told that to a man from the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Did you hear that from him? I said: Yes. He said: I bear witness that I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There is no man who repents before his soul rattles in his throat but Allah accepts his repentance."
"598","His saying, exalted: 'And if you intend to replace a wife in place of a wife, and you gave one of them a qintar, take nothing from it; would you take it by slander and clear sin?' 598 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Fudayl ibn Iyad narrated, from Layth, from Mujahid, he said: The qintar is seventy thousand dinars."
"599","599 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Khalid ibn Abdillah narrated, from Awf, from al-Hasan, he said: The qintar is the blood-money of a free man."
"600","His saying, exalted: 'Forbidden to you are your mothers, your daughters, your sisters, your paternal aunts, your maternal aunts, your brother's daughters, your sister's daughters, your milk-mothers who suckled you, your milk-sisters, your wives' mothers, your stepdaughters in your care from your wives you entered, and if you did not enter them there is no blame on you, and the wives of your sons from your loins, and that you gather two sisters except what passed; indeed Allah is Forgiving, Merciful.' 600 - Sa'id narrated, he said: Ismail ibn Ibrahim narrated, from Sa'id al-Jurayri, from Hayyan ibn Umayr, he said: Ibn Abbas said: Seven by marriage and seven by blood, and there is prohibited by suckling what is prohibited by blood."
